WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:17.580
Tim Ferriss: Wade, welcome&nbsp;
to the show. It is an honor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:22.740
to have you. I've been meaning to reach out&nbsp;
for a very long time and I appreciate you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.440
carving out the time in your schedule.
Wade Davis: Well, thanks very much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.480
Tim. It's great to be with you.
Tim Ferriss: And I suppose I should just say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.240
as a bit of context, the catalyst for reaching out&nbsp;
was not one of your many TED Talks, although I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.080
listened to many. It was not One River, although&nbsp;
I'm familiar with that as well. It was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:48.840
being gifted The Wayfinders by a friend of mine.&nbsp;
And I suppose just as a way of setting the stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:54.900
if you wouldn't mind, could you explain the basic&nbsp;
intent of that book and the lectures that preceded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.900 --> 00:01:01.440
it? And I'm curious, since it was published&nbsp;
sometime ago, if there is one story that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:08.940
wish people would become familiar with, or a&nbsp;
chapter that you wish you could compel many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.380
many people to read at this point in time?
Wade Davis: Yeah, well that's a wonderful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:20.940
beginning, Tim. The Wayfinders was a book&nbsp;
put together in a really wonderful tradition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:27.240
in Canada called the CBC Massey Lectures.&nbsp;
And it's a fantastic event where once each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.500
year they pick what they call a public&nbsp;
intellectual, and you're asked to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:37.080
five different talks in five different cities&nbsp;
before live audiences. Those talks are recorded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:44.940
for broadcast on the radio three times during this&nbsp;
coming year, and then the lectures themselves are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.500
wrapped up into a book. And it's kind of an&nbsp;
interesting thing because as opposed to most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:55.500
public speaking, you've got a lot of things going&nbsp;
on. You're recording for live radio, you've got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:00.540
live audience, and you're also essentially&nbsp;
delivering the lecture that's already been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:05.460
published and often is in the lap of the audience&nbsp;
if they've bought the book. But it's a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:11.100
tradition. Martin Luther King gave them, I was the&nbsp;
first anthropologist since Claude Lévi-Strauss,&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:17.880
The Wayfinders has a very conversational&nbsp;
style and I think that's one reason it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.800
been quite successful, and particularly for&nbsp;
college students. And the basis of the book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:29.100
was really the mission that I had at the National&nbsp;
Geographic. I was very fortunate to be recruited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:34.080
as the first class of what the Geographic&nbsp;
was calling their explorers and residents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.920
which is kind of an odd term because&nbsp;
none of us were ever in residence.&nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:45.300
They wanted to demonstrate, personify&nbsp;
that they didn't just report science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:50.280
they generated science. And so that they&nbsp;
recruited seven individuals, Jane Goodall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:56.340
Bob Ballard, who found the Titanic, Sylvia Earle,&nbsp;
the great oceanographer, a host of incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:01.920
characters, Johan Reinhard, the high-altitude&nbsp;
archeologist who found the Ice Maiden, a perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:08.580
preserved Incan mummy on Llullaillaco. And&nbsp;
they recruited me as a cultural anthropologist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:13.680
and it was very much part of a conservation&nbsp;
mission. In the second hundred years, having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:19.140
told you about the world, National Geographic was&nbsp;
going to help you save the world. And my mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:25.140
as defined in my contract, was to change the&nbsp;
way the world viewed and valued culture in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:31.620
decade. And the way to do that was not through&nbsp;
politics or polemics, but through storytelling.&nbsp;

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:38.460
Because storytellers, as you well know Tim,&nbsp;
change the world. And what we were trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:44.160
share with the public was kind of the fundamental&nbsp;
revelation of anthropology. The idea that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.500
other peoples of the world aren't failed attempts&nbsp;
of being you. They're not failed attempts of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:55.980
modern. Every culture is a unique answer to a&nbsp;
fundamental question: what does it mean to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:00.660
human and alive? And when the peoples of&nbsp;
the world answer that, they do so in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:06.300
7,000 different voices of humanity. And all&nbsp;
those answers kind of collectively become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:13.680
our human repertoire. And so we also wanted to&nbsp;
draw people's attention to the kind of haunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:19.260
fact that out of those 7,000 languages&nbsp;
spoken the day you, Tim, and I were born,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:27.060
by absolute academic consensus, half are not being&nbsp;
taught to children. Which means they're moribund,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:33.180
on the brink of extinction, if not exhaustion.
And that means in effect that we're living through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:37.140
an era where half of humanity's&nbsp;
intellectual, social, spiritual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.600
even ecological knowledge is at risk. And at&nbsp;
the same time, and this is the amazing thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:48.960
we're living through an era where geneticists have&nbsp;
finally proven it to be true what philosophers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.240
and poets have always dreamt to be true, that we&nbsp;
really are all brothers and sisters. And I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:05:00.720
mean that in the spirit of hippie ethnography. I&nbsp;
mean that studies of the human genome have shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:07.140
without doubt that the genetic endowment of&nbsp;
humanity is a continuum. Race is a total fiction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:13.140
We're all cut from the same genetic cloth. We're&nbsp;
all descendants of Africa, including those of us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:18.480
who walked out of the ancient continent 65,000&nbsp;
years ago. But here's the astonishing thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.980
If we're cut from the same genetic cloth,&nbsp;
by definition, we share the same genius.&nbsp;

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:30.600
And how that genius is expressed is simply a&nbsp;
matter of choice and cultural adaptation. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:37.080
so there is no hierarchy in culture. That old&nbsp;
Victorian idea that we went from the savage to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:42.540
the barbarian to the civilized, to the Strand&nbsp;
of London. That Victorian societies sat at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:47.640
apex of a pyramid that went down to the so-called&nbsp;
primitives of the world, absolutely ridiculed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:52.980
modern science, shown to be an artifact of the&nbsp;
19th century, irrelevant to our lives today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:59.160
and as distant from those lives as the idea of&nbsp;
clergymen in that era who believed the Earth was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:06.360
only 6,000 years old. So then the question is, how&nbsp;
do you share this? How do you reveal this kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:13.020
wondrous thing about culture to the world? You&nbsp;
know, you have to show, you can't tell. Polemics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:22.140
are never persuasive. So the reason that book, The&nbsp;
Wayfinders, it tells the story of the expeditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:30.120
that we did to share this message across the&nbsp;
world. And so we deliberately, and it wasn't easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:36.660
we didn't want to simply go out as so many&nbsp;
ethnographic filmmakers tend to do, celebrating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:44.460
the exoticism of the other. We really wanted to go&nbsp;
to places where the beliefs and practices revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:50.040
this extraordinary universal truth. And I think if&nbsp;
you asked which was the most extraordinary of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:54.623
and it would have to be the Polynesian Wayfinders.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that blew my mind.&nbsp;

00:06:54.623 --> 00:06:58.020
Wade Davis: The type of story of the book. I mean,&nbsp;
this is just an amazing thing if you think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:07.380
it. Even today, members of the Polynesian Voyaging&nbsp;
Society can name 250 stars in the night sky. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:13.560
can sense the presence of distant atolls of&nbsp;
islands beyond the visible horizon just by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:19.740
watching the reverberation of waves across the&nbsp;
hull of their sacred canoe, The Hōkūleʻa, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:25.560
great vessel that is a symbol of this Polynesian&nbsp;
renaissance. In the darkness in the hull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:31.320
they can distinguish as many as five different&nbsp;
sea swells, again, moving through the water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:37.440
distinguishing those caused by local weather&nbsp;
disturbances from those that pulsate across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:43.260
the ocean and can be followed with the ease with&nbsp;
which terrestrial explorer would follow a river&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:48.420
to the sea. And each of these chapters in that&nbsp;
book, Tim, and the subjects also became films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:52.680
of course that we did for the Geographic.&nbsp;
I kind of tried to find, not a punchline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:58.800
but a kind of line that would sum it all up. And&nbsp;
so with Polynesia, it was very simple. If you took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:04.500
all of the genius that allowed us to put a man&nbsp;
on the moon and applied it to an understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:11.280
of the ocean, what you would get is Polynesia.
Tim Ferriss: I found it so striking, and I may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:17.640
using the wrong terms, that the captain and the&nbsp;
navigator were two entirely distinct functions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:28.200
And that's a great example to me of perhaps just&nbsp;
a fundamentally different way of viewing seafaring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:34.680
when you come from a western lens. And I really&nbsp;
enjoyed that book. I encourage everybody to pick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:41.280
it up. And if it's okay with you, I would love to&nbsp;
actually segue to another culture, another group.&nbsp;

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:45.660
Wade Davis: But Tim, before we leave Polynesia,&nbsp;
let me just add one thing that just to clarify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:52.020
things for your listeners. The amazing thing&nbsp;
about this tradition was that it was based on dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:57.960
reckoning, which means that you only know where&nbsp;
you are by remembering how you got there. And it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:03.360
was the impossibility of doing that that kept&nbsp;
most European transports hugging the shores of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:08.340
continents until the British solved the problem of&nbsp;
longitude with the invention of the chronometer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:16.080
But we know that 10 centuries before Christ, from&nbsp;
an ancient civilization called Lapita, the ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:23.100
ancestors of the Polynesians set sail into the&nbsp;
rising sun. And this idea of dead reckoning means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:30.240
and back to your navigator, why he's not running&nbsp;
the ship, because he or she must sit monk-like on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:36.600
the back of the vessel, remembering every shift of&nbsp;
the wind, every tack, every sign of the sun, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:44.640
moon, the stars, the birds, the salinity in the&nbsp;
water, every one of these empirical observations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:51.840
and the order of their acquisition. And if that&nbsp;
memory chain is broken, the voyage can end in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:59.220
a disaster. And all of this has to be done by an&nbsp;
individual who lives in a civilization that lacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:07.980
the written word. So all of this has to be placed&nbsp;
in memory over a three-and four-week voyage. Think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:12.660
about that. Tell me that is not a form of genius.
Tim Ferriss: Not just a form of genius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:19.020
but a form of endurance. Almost beyond belief.&nbsp;
How many hours of sleep on average over the voyage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:24.180
per day, or per night with that navigator get?
Wade Davis: It's a great question because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:31.320
kind of catnap in a way, but they can't really&nbsp;
do much more than just catnapping. It's funny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:39.300
this idea of sleep. We're so wired to the clock&nbsp;
that we feel that we've somehow done something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:45.960
filthy or nasty if we only sleep four hours.&nbsp;
But many societies around — I spend a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:51.000
time in the Arctic, and one of the things that's&nbsp;
fascinating is that winter is a time for sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:59.580
the air of perpetual darkness. And summer, the&nbsp;
light is luminous all day long and it's not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:05.520
appropriate to sleep. You kind of catnap with&nbsp;
your dogs, but there's just too much to be done.&nbsp;

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:14.700
Tim Ferriss: So let's shift just a bit or&nbsp;
maybe entirely to the Kogi peoples of Northern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:21.720
Colombia. You are deeply, deeply familiar&nbsp;
and intimate with Colombia and its people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:27.000
I had the opportunity through a&nbsp;
mutual acquaintance, Dr. Mark Plotkin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:36.480
to meet a mamo and small group of Kogi, but it&nbsp;
was a very cursory experience and they were very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:45.540
select with their words and communication. Could&nbsp;
you describe the Kogi people of Northern Colombia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:52.860
and perhaps just paint a picture for people&nbsp;
of what that culture and what those peoples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.880 --> 00:12:00.000
look like? Because I find them to stand&nbsp;
out, at least for me, amongst the cultures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.140
I've been exposed to in a number of ways.
Wade Davis: Yeah, it's truly remarkable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:10.920
They live in Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta. The&nbsp;
highest coastal mountain range on Earth that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:18.180
soars out of the Caribbean coastal plain to about&nbsp;
20,000 feet. There are four indigenous groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:24.900
the Kogi you mentioned, the Wiwa, the Arhuacos,&nbsp;
and the Kankuamo. The Kankuamo in the 19th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:36.420
kind of cut a Faustian deal with the greater&nbsp;
Colombian society and endured a great deal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:40.260
assimilation. And they're kind of struggling&nbsp;
to get back to their traditional ways. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:46.320
the other three societies remain absolutely&nbsp;
extraordinary. And in a bloodstained continent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:53.100
you can almost say they were never conquered&nbsp;
by the Spanish fully. They are descendants of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:59.100
an ancient civilization called Tairona, which&nbsp;
suffered immensely in the first decades of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:06.000
Spanish conquest. And the survivors fled into this&nbsp;
mountain massif where they lived almost in total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:12.180
isolation, very little reference to them in the&nbsp;
colonial documents, for 200, if not 300 years.&nbsp;

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:19.020
And it's almost as if they had suffered so much&nbsp;
that they made a kind of collective vow never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:24.480
to screw up again. And I think that accounts for&nbsp;
their intense religiosity. Many people call them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:31.920
the Tibetans of South America, but they live&nbsp;
to this day inspired by a ritual priesthood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:38.460
the mamos, the sun priests. And the training for&nbsp;
the priesthood is extraordinary. It was first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:44.040
reported in the 1940s by Reichel-Dolmatoff that&nbsp;
the acolytes were taken away from their families&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:50.280
at the age of two and three, and then sequestered&nbsp;
in the shadowy world of darkness for 18 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:56.940
during which time they absorbed the religious&nbsp;
beliefs of their society. And it's their sincere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:03.720
convection that those beliefs, those rituals,&nbsp;
those prayers literally maintain the cosmic, or we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:10.140
might say the ecological balance of the world. And&nbsp;
according to Reichel, after 18 years in which the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:16.740
world only existed as an abstraction, the young&nbsp;
acolyte was taken out and taken on a journey.&nbsp;

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:23.280
And for the first time in his life at the&nbsp;
age of 18 or 19, he saw the horizon. He saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:29.400
the mountains, he saw the sun, and suddenly the&nbsp;
priest who has trained them all these years says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:36.180
"You know it's that beautiful, as I've promised&nbsp;
you. It's yours to protect." Now this was almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:43.440
a fable within anthropology because Reichel&nbsp;
never saw the ritual. He never went on one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:49.560
those pilgrimages to the Heart of the World. Then&nbsp;
an amazing thing happened. I first lived with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:55.260
Arhuacos when I was a lad of 19 and 20.&nbsp;
In fact, when you mentioned, it's amazing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:59.820
I've now been close with them for&nbsp;
almost 50 years. They, in fact, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:07.140
Arhuacos call me their Mamo Occidental. I was&nbsp;
once with President Santos, the Nobel laureate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:12.300
the first time he ever visited Nabusimake. And&nbsp;
the mamos had asked me to be there to welcome him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:18.360
and I hitched a ride in the presidential plane.
And when we got to the community, there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:24.240
kind of formal ceremony in which the president&nbsp;
introduced his invitados, his ministers and so on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:28.440
And he got around to me and he couldn't have been&nbsp;
more generous with his praise and his kind words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:32.760
but he was interrupted by one of the mamos who&nbsp;
said, "You don't have to tell us about that guy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:38.760
He's our ambassador in North America." And so&nbsp;
I have a very wonderful relationship to them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:46.440
But here's what was extraordinary. In one wintry&nbsp;
day in Washington, the Colombian ambassador then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:52.500
Carolina Barco, a good friend of mine, turned up&nbsp;
at my office at the Geographic with a political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:59.400
leader, Danilo Villafañe and three mamos, one&nbsp;
from each of the three cultures. Wiwa, Kogi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:09.900
and Arhuaco. They were there because the BBC had&nbsp;
made a film and the Arhuacos felt they hadn't had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:15.540
their say and they wanted to make their own film.
So they'd come to me and as I'm looking at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:21.060
guy, Danilo, he looks so much like an old&nbsp;
friend of mine. So I pulled out a book of mine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:27.180
One River, which happened to have a photograph&nbsp;
in the frontispiece of one of the chapters. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:34.860
showed him the photo and that was Danilo's father,&nbsp;
and who was murdered by the paramilitaries. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:40.200
I said to Danilo, "Son, you don't remember, but&nbsp;
when you were a little infant, I carried you on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:45.840
my back for weeks up and down the mountains with&nbsp;
your father." And he was so touched by that and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:50.940
the connection was so strong that he invited us&nbsp;
to do what I would've thought was the impossible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.940 --> 00:16:57.900
To actually go along on a journey to the Heart&nbsp;
of the World and make a film about this idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.900 --> 00:17:06.840
and the pilgrimage. And the idea is very simple.
As you come out of the sacred temple — and what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.840 --> 00:17:12.540
we discovered is they don't stay 18 years in&nbsp;
the darkness, but 18 years in seclusion around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:19.560
the temple and a ritual diet, not seeing women.&nbsp;
And then they do go from the temple to the ice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:24.780
and from the ice back to the sea, and from the&nbsp;
sea back to the temple, completing this sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:32.160
sacred devotional pilgrimage of the divine.&nbsp;
And we made that film. Unfortunately at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:37.800
very penultimate stage of the pilgrimage, we were&nbsp;
kind of ambushed by the FARC and we had to escape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:45.000
and turn our cameras over to one of the Wiwa&nbsp;
lads that we had trained in cinematography.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:50.160
And with incredible skill, he finished those&nbsp;
segments of the film. So we actually had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:56.340
entire pilgrimage documented. But I think there's&nbsp;
a bigger point to make about the elder brothers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.340 --> 00:18:01.200
as they call themselves. They dismiss all of us&nbsp;
who have ruined the world as the younger brothers.&nbsp;

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:06.300
And these are societies that do not view the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:13.860
through an extractive paradigm. They do not think&nbsp;
that the world is just a kind of a stage set upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:21.240
which only the human drama unfolds. They don't&nbsp;
buy into the old Descartesian idea that all that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:26.520
exists is mind and matter, and that only things&nbsp;
that can be measured can exist. That whole kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:32.760
of idea that we develop in the European tradition&nbsp;
that has now become so dominant, so powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:39.360
so ubiquitous, but it is not the norm. It is&nbsp;
highly anomalous. Most societies interact with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:45.660
the natural world through the kind of metaphor of&nbsp;
reciprocity. Some idea that the Earth gives its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:54.360
bounty to us. We owe our fidelity to the Earth.&nbsp;
And that's very much how the mamos see their role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.360 --> 00:19:01.140
as representatives of the natural order of things.&nbsp;
It's funny, when I was first asked by them to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:07.980
and be there, when President Santos arrived, one&nbsp;
of my close friends, a man called Mamo Camilo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:11.940
said to me something very profound.
He said, "Peace won't matter,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:18.720
and this is after 50 years of Colombia's horrific&nbsp;
war. "Peace won't matter if it's only an excuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:24.480
for the three sides to come together to maintain a&nbsp;
war against nature. It's time for us to make peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:33.120
with the entire natural world." And as we flew up&nbsp;
to Valledupar on the presidential jet from Bogota,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:40.020
all of the president's aides were peppering him&nbsp;
with statistics for his speech in the community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:45.300
that was going to be broadcast internationally.&nbsp;
And I kind of sheepishly put up my hand and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:52.020
said, in Spanish, "President Santos, for the&nbsp;
mamos, statistics don't matter a rat's ass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:57.840
What they care about is what's in your heart."&nbsp;
And then I told him what Mamo Camilo had said.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:03.720
And President Santos, an incredibly wonderful&nbsp;
man, incorporated that and made that the theme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:11.280
of his speech that went out that day to the world.
Tim Ferriss: I was very struck by, and I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:18.000
want to spend too much time on this, but I'll just&nbsp;
say, struck by how central pagamientos — offerings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:25.920
and payments — seem to be to certainly the mamos,&nbsp;
but broadly speaking, the Kogi. And I appreciate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:30.900
all the context that you just provided. I would&nbsp;
also love you, and we could spend five hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:39.180
just discussing what I'm about to bring up. But&nbsp;
I also would love to ask you about coca. So many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:43.380
people are familiar with coca as a leaf that is&nbsp;
chewed or something that has turned into cocaine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:50.820
But could you talk a bit about mambe? So&nbsp;
this is a word and something that has come up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:55.680
in my radius a number of times, but what is mambe?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.680 --> 00:21:02.280
Where is it used? How is it prepared?
Wade Davis: Coca is a generic term for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:07.800
two different cultivated species and four&nbsp;
different varieties that have been exploited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:14.160
by people in South America, perhaps as long&nbsp;
as 8,000 years, certainly 5,000 years. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:22.680
should say that coca is to cocaine what potatoes&nbsp;
are to vodka. And the two main types of coca,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:30.600
one is called Colombian, that's Erythroxylum&nbsp;
novogranatense, and the other is a classic coca&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:39.000
of the Southern Andes, of Cusco and Lapas, that is&nbsp;
Erythroxylum coca. Now in pre-Columbian times, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:46.860
variety of that was taken down the Amazon into the&nbsp;
jungles of the northwest Amazon. And this variety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:54.960
which is known as Erythroxylum coca variety ipadu&nbsp;
is cultivated vegetatively, not from seed. It also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.960 --> 00:22:01.500
has half the alkaloid concentration. And so in a&nbsp;
very interesting way, the peoples of the Anaconda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:08.040
all these extraordinary societies, the Barasana,&nbsp;
Macuna, Tukano, Cubeo, Ticuna, I mean there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:13.500
scores of these extraordinary cultures.
They've learned to take the leaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:22.920
roast them over a clay griddle, and then rather&nbsp;
than taking the leaf orally and mixing some kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:29.040
of alkaline with it, baking soda or limestone&nbsp;
or ashes of certain plants, as you'll see in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:36.120
the mountains of southern Peru, they add the ash&nbsp;
of the leaves of the tree known as yaruma. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:40.860
then they pound the two together until you&nbsp;
get a very fine powder, which becomes even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:48.540
more fine when sifted through palm fiber. It's the&nbsp;
consistency of talc. And with mambe then, you take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:58.020
the actual coca with a bone like this. This is a&nbsp;
mambe bone right here. And you put the wad onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.020 --> 00:23:05.880
your mouth and you let the saliva kind of soften&nbsp;
it and you don't really talk or breathe, or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:12.240
whole works will just explode as a green cloud.&nbsp;
And as it's moist, you then lift it up as a quid.&nbsp;

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:19.320
And the advantage of course is that by taking coca&nbsp;
in this way, you absorb the entire plant and thus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:24.180
all the nutrients. I mean one of the, just be&nbsp;
before I just finish that, then the other coca,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:31.320
the coca of the Kogi that we talked about, that&nbsp;
is Erythroxylum novogranatense, it's the coca of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.320 --> 00:23:38.520
Colombia. And that coca was taken down the coast&nbsp;
to Trujillo in the northern desert of Peru. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:44.040
that became the preferred coca of the Inca.&nbsp;
It's got a little wintergreen oil in it, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:50.940
Erythroxylum novogranatense variety truxillense,&nbsp;
and that of course was a preferred coca that to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:56.580
this day, Coca-Cola imports each year by the&nbsp;
ton allowing their beverage to really be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.580 --> 00:24:02.940
real thing. The fascinating story, Tim, is&nbsp;
that I worked with Tim Plowman, a protege of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:10.080
my professor Schultes, Mark's professor, in the&nbsp;
'70s, and we were charged to work out the botany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:16.740
the ethnobotany, the ethnography of coca.
And at that time we thought that the coca&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:23.700
of Colombia, for classic botanical reasons, was&nbsp;
derived from the coca of Peru. But now that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:23.700 --> 00:24:30.300
have DNA, we see a greater story. It turns&nbsp;
out that these two sacred plants used for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:36.060
8,000 years, revered as a divine leaf of&nbsp;
immortality by every culture in the Andes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:43.260
all come from the same wild ancestor, a&nbsp;
species known as Erythroxylum gracilipes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:48.420
which grows along the eastern flanks of the&nbsp;
Andes. Now that may seem like arcane botany&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.420 --> 00:24:56.220
talk to many of our listeners, but it's actually&nbsp;
a miracle. Because to have two revered plants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.220 --> 00:25:03.120
independently domesticated through a process of&nbsp;
artificial selection, thousands of miles apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:09.720
and yet each deemed to be sacred essence&nbsp;
of the divine, is unheard of. No precedent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:17.220
in all of the history of botany and of human&nbsp;
cultures. And so this is the richness of coca.&nbsp;

00:25:17.220 --> 00:25:25.140
Now, the extraordinary thing is that the&nbsp;
efforts to eradicate coca fields began 50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:31.620
years before there was a cocaine problem. In&nbsp;
the 1920s, physicians in Lima in particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:37.080
looked up into the Andes and their concern for&nbsp;
the well-being of Andean people was matched in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:42.720
intensity only by their ignorance of Andean life.&nbsp;
And when they saw illiteracy, poor sanitation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:50.700
one social pathology after the other, they had&nbsp;
to find a cause. And because issues of economics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:57.000
land distribution, inequity came too close&nbsp;
to challenging the bourgeois foundations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.000 --> 00:26:02.280
of their lives in Lima, they had to find&nbsp;
the evil source. And the source was coca.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:07.380
And they blamed coca for every ill in&nbsp;
the Andes. And through all those years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.380 --> 00:26:14.940
these doctors and physicians and nutritionists&nbsp;
never did the obvious, a nutritional study to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:21.060
show just what this plant actually had in it.&nbsp;
And when we finally did that in the mid-1970s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.060 --> 00:26:28.080
Andrew Weil, Tim Plowman, Jim Duke at the USDA,&nbsp;
we discovered, horrified our backers at the DEA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:34.020
and the US government, because it turns out&nbsp;
that coca has a tiny amount of cocaine in it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:41.400
absorbed benignly as a mild stimulant by the mucus&nbsp;
membrane of the mouth, absolutely without harm.&nbsp;

00:26:41.400 --> 00:26:48.840
The plant also has more calcium than any plant&nbsp;
ever studied, perfect for a traditional diet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.840 --> 00:26:54.720
without a dairy product. It also is chock full&nbsp;
of vitamins. It even has enzymes that enhance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.720 --> 00:27:00.120
the body's ability to digest carbohydrate&nbsp;
at high elevation, making it perfect for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:05.160
potato-based diet. So in one simple nutritional&nbsp;
study that could have been done at any time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:13.260
we put into stark profile these hideous efforts&nbsp;
that are underway, to this day, to destroy the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:17.160
traditional fields. And we showed that this&nbsp;
was a plant that had been used with no evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:23.700
of toxicity, let alone addiction, for at least&nbsp;
5,000 years. And so one of our big efforts today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:31.800
is to decouple coca from cocaine and create a&nbsp;
nutraceutical market for the plant that will give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:39.780
a legal market for the 150,000 families in&nbsp;
Colombia alone that depend on cultivating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:47.340
coca for their survival. And also through taxes&nbsp;
may just give Colombia the revenue necessary to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:54.900
pay the cost of peace, having drained its treasury&nbsp;
for 50 years to pay the cost of a war only made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:54.900 --> 00:28:02.220
possible by the sordid profits of prohibition.
Tim Ferriss: So we may come back to coca, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.220 --> 00:28:08.280
as you're discussing the nutritional profile, it&nbsp;
makes me also think of the role that coffee serves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:16.740
in some populations in the world. But I would love&nbsp;
to go to some well-trodden ground, and this is out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:22.200
of personal curiosity and also because I think&nbsp;
that many people will want to hear more of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:27.660
background here. And then we're going to probably&nbsp;
come to discuss a number of mentors of yours.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.660 --> 00:28:36.900
But I would love to hear you expand on TTX and&nbsp;
Datura stramonium, if I'm pronouncing that right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.900 --> 00:28:45.720
and how you came across these two in combination.&nbsp;
Because I think Datura stramonium, also known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:45.720 --> 00:28:48.283
Jimson weed, if I'm not getting that —
Wade Davis: Yeah, that's right.&nbsp;

00:28:48.283 --> 00:28:53.400
Tim Ferriss: — incorrect, grows right in&nbsp;
my driveway in Texas. It's found in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:58.440
sorts of places. TTX, a little less&nbsp;
so. But could you just provide the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:03.060
background on where you came across these two?
Wade Davis: Sure. This is an incredible story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:03.060 --> 00:29:09.900
a kind of an assignment of a lifetime that&nbsp;
would completely change my trajectory. I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:15.960
done a lot of work in the Amazon, three years&nbsp;
in fact, through the Andes and the Amazon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:22.560
And I had studied anthropology, but I never really&nbsp;
understood the real message of anthropology until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:28.920
I went to Haiti. I'll explain that in a moment.
But what happened is that a very well-known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:34.080
psychopharmacologist by the name of Nathan Kline,&nbsp;
psychopharmacology being the study of the action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:39.960
of drugs on the brain, had been going to Haiti&nbsp;
for many years. The psychiatric institute bore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:46.080
his name. He had set it up. A close colleague&nbsp;
of his at McGill University, Heinz Lehmann,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:51.660
had a former student, Lamarque Douyon, who is&nbsp;
now the director of that psychiatric institute.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.660 --> 00:29:58.860
And Douyon was fascinated by the Haitian zombie&nbsp;
phenomena, and of course by folkloric belief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:04.380
the zombie is a living dead. It's an individual&nbsp;
who has had their soul stolen by sorcery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:11.520
kind of propelled into a perpetual state of&nbsp;
purgatory, said to be associated with enslavement.&nbsp;

00:30:12.720 --> 00:30:16.440
And this was sort of something very much&nbsp;
from the realm of the phantasmagoric.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:21.900
But Douyon had been paying attention and&nbsp;
investigating every case that came his way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:21.900 --> 00:30:28.680
And finally he discovered this remarkable&nbsp;
story of a man called Clairvius Narcisse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:28.680 --> 00:30:36.300
who in the early 1960s had been misdiagnosed&nbsp;
dead, or he been diagnosed dead by two physicians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.300 --> 00:30:42.420
both American-trained and one an the American, in&nbsp;
the Albert Schweitzer Hospital in Central Haiti,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:42.420 --> 00:30:48.120
an American-directed institution that keeps&nbsp;
impeccable records. And this man claiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.120 --> 00:30:54.960
to be Narcisse later walked into his village in&nbsp;
about 1980, '81, claiming to be the long-lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.960 --> 00:31:00.480
brother. The family member had no doubt, but they&nbsp;
immediately told him to get lost and he had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.480 --> 00:31:08.160
escape to the police station for his own safety.&nbsp;
And when Douyon looked into this, he was able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:14.940
secure death certificates. Scotland Yard verified&nbsp;
with their forensic expertise, the fingerprints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.940 --> 00:31:21.360
that belonged to the sister of the deceased.
There were a score of lines of evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:21.360 --> 00:31:27.600
that suggests that this man clearly had been&nbsp;
misdiagnosed dead and somehow turned up in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:32.160
realm of living. And in fact, Douyon went to the&nbsp;
family members and put together a questionnaire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:39.480
of intimate information of the family background,&nbsp;
all of which this man answered correctly. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:39.480 --> 00:31:48.420
bottom line is that Douyon and Lehmann and Dr.&nbsp;
Kline went public saying they felt they had found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:56.880
the first zombie. Now that drew their attention&nbsp;
to reports of a folk poison that was said to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:56.880 --> 00:32:00.720
bring on a state of apparent death so profound&nbsp;
it could fool a physician. Now this poison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:07.200
wasn't just mentioned in travelers' accounts and&nbsp;
missionary memoirs and in ethnographic reports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:13.560
it was specifically mentioned in the penal code&nbsp;
of the country. But Douyon had not been able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:18.960
find the poison. He hadn't secured a formula&nbsp;
of it. And this was key to the whole question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:24.180
of the Haitian zombie. It was either something&nbsp;
from the realm of fantasy or if it was real,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:29.280
there had to be a natural product. And if that&nbsp;
product existed, that could make someone appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:35.580
to be dead such that they could come back in&nbsp;
the realm of living undamaged, that had huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:42.480
potential medical applications as Kline saw it.
So they came to Harvard, Schultes said he was too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:48.060
old to go, but he said he knew someone who could&nbsp;
do the job. And that's how I was hired to go down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.060 --> 00:32:55.020
to Haiti to secure this poison. Now remember,&nbsp;
I wasn't looking for a poison that could kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.020 --> 00:33:01.080
people. Lots of things can do that. I was looking&nbsp;
for something much more rare, which was a poison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:01.080 --> 00:33:05.940
that could bring someone to the state of apparent&nbsp;
death so profound, it could fool a physician and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:05.940 --> 00:33:15.120
yet the victim could survive. And so I did what&nbsp;
one does. I contacted a sorcerer who was very — he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:21.300
had been described by the BBC as the incarnation&nbsp;
of evil. He was nothing of the sort. He had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:21.300 --> 00:33:26.940
whorehouse and a bunch of Dominican women and he&nbsp;
had been a junior member of the Tonton Macoutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:34.620
But I was able to establish, through a little&nbsp;
bit of theatrics, a good relationship with him.&nbsp;

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:38.400
Tim Ferriss: What was the group&nbsp;
that you mentioned? I'm not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:44.580
familiar with it. A junior member of what group?
Wade Davis: The Tonton Macoutes. Tonton in Creole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:50.340
means "Uncle." Macoute means "shoulder bag."&nbsp;
This was the nickname for the volunteers for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:50.340 --> 00:33:56.700
national security. The militia that was set up by&nbsp;
François Duvalier in the wake of his presidential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:57.300 --> 00:34:04.260
election in 1957. And this is a pivotal part&nbsp;
of the story because Duvalier was the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:09.540
president in a hundred years to say that Vodou&nbsp;
was legitimate religion. He had Vodou temples in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:15.000
the presidential palace. He wore the costume&nbsp;
of Baron Samedi, the guardian of the dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:21.720
of the graveyard. He played Vodou like a&nbsp;
charm. And he used the secret societies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:22.860 --> 00:34:29.700
as his base of power. And from them, as my&nbsp;
research would discover, he created this notorious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:29.700 --> 00:34:35.580
force, the Tonton Macoutes. And it means that if&nbsp;
you misbehave, they will come and take you away in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:45.540
their shoulder bag. And so I went out with Marcel&nbsp;
after this bit of theater that we did to — it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:45.540 --> 00:34:47.580
kind of funny. I'll tell you about it sometime —
Tim Ferriss: I want to hear about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:50.280
theater. Yeah. How do you develop a rapport?
Wade Davis: Well, what happened was I went to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:50.280 --> 00:34:56.520
him with a good friend of mine, Max Beauvoir, who,&nbsp;
when he died, was sort of heralded as a Pope of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:56.520 --> 00:35:02.580
Vodou, an amazing kind of conduit for the outside&nbsp;
world to understand Vodou. And again, I should say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:02.580 --> 00:35:09.840
right off the top, Tim, that we have this idea of&nbsp;
Vodou from the movies. It couldn't be more wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:14.880
And just think for a moment, if we were asked&nbsp;
to name the great religions of the world, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:20.880
continent would we leave out? Sub-Saharan Africa.&nbsp;
And of course, Vodou is not a black magic cult.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:20.880 --> 00:35:27.120
It's a Fon word from Dahomey or Benin that means&nbsp;
"spirit" or "god." It's just the distillation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:27.120 --> 00:35:32.520
very profound religious ideas that came over&nbsp;
during the era of slavery and then became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:34.080 --> 00:35:39.240
transformed within the soil of a new world.&nbsp;
That's why you have Hoodoo in the American South,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:40.200 --> 00:35:49.020
Candomblé in Brazil, Obeah in Jamaica, and so on —&nbsp;
Santería in the DR, and of course, Vodou in Haiti.&nbsp;

00:35:49.020 --> 00:35:55.200
And Vodou took a particular strong form in&nbsp;
Haiti because as opposed to the other countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.200 --> 00:36:00.780
I mentioned, Haiti was an independent Black&nbsp;
country, the only one in the world for a century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.780 --> 00:36:08.400
gained its independence in 1804. And at that&nbsp;
time, much of its population, the slaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:13.980
had literally been born in Africa. So in many&nbsp;
ways, you can almost argue that Haiti's more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:21.480
Africa than Africa itself at this point. But at&nbsp;
any rate, I saw Marcel and he made me the powder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:21.480 --> 00:36:27.900
but I knew the way he made it, the ingredients,&nbsp;
that it was kind of bogus. And so instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:33.000
telling him that, I doubled what I had&nbsp;
promised to pay him, and as I left the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:37.200
hounfour, the temple, I mentioned I was&nbsp;
going to try it on an enemy I had in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:37.200 --> 00:36:42.420
capital and I'd let him know how it worked.
And then with a deliberate piece of theater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:42.420 --> 00:36:48.480
Max Beauvoir and I stormed back a week later and&nbsp;
screamed and yelled at him that we had nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:52.620
gotten killed, that his powder was worthless,&nbsp;
so he couldn't do a thing. And of course he,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.620 --> 00:36:56.580
then, got furious and he went into the inner&nbsp;
sanctum in the temple and came out with a bottle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:56.580 --> 00:37:01.500
a little vial. And he said in Creole, "If&nbsp;
you don't think I know how to make poison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.500 --> 00:37:06.360
drink this, you won't walk out of here alive."&nbsp;
And then all the Dominican girls started going,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:06.360 --> 00:37:12.000
"Bwè! Bwè! Drink, drink, drink!" And it&nbsp;
was a bit tense. And so I said to Marcel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:12.000 --> 00:37:15.660
"Look. It's not that you don't know how to make&nbsp;
good poison. I came all this way because I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:15.660 --> 00:37:20.340
you can. I'm just saying what you made me is&nbsp;
garbage. And if you give me garbage, you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.340 --> 00:37:24.060
never see me again. But if you give me the real&nbsp;
thing, you might make a lot of money from us."&nbsp;

00:37:24.060 --> 00:37:28.800
And then I walked out. And I went back the next&nbsp;
day and the proper ingredients were drying on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:28.800 --> 00:37:34.020
the clothesline. And then we went into the inner&nbsp;
sanctum, the temple, and he took a bottle of raw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:34.020 --> 00:37:40.320
alcohol with human remains in it and all kinds of&nbsp;
animals and gore of one sort or the other, and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.320 --> 00:37:45.420
handed it to me and I took a big drink and handed&nbsp;
it right back to him. And he laughed. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:45.420 --> 00:37:51.720
it was the first time of many times that a Haitian&nbsp;
would say to me, "Ki kalite Blan say ou?" — "What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:51.720 --> 00:37:55.320
kind of White are you, anyway?" And so&nbsp;
that was the beginning of my relationship.&nbsp;

00:37:55.320 --> 00:38:00.768
Tim Ferriss: Were you not&nbsp;
worried about drinking that?&nbsp;

00:38:00.768 --> 00:38:02.220
Wade Davis: No.
Tim Ferriss: What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:06.240
was the expectation of the ingredients?
Wade Davis: Well, it was just a ritual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.240 --> 00:38:08.688
vessel of magical things.
Tim Ferriss: I see.&nbsp;

00:38:08.688 --> 00:38:13.260
Wade Davis: He's a sorcerer, a negative&nbsp;
priest, and it's all been pickled in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.260 --> 00:38:19.260
sugar cane alcohol. Part of it is, it's not like&nbsp;
macho. It's actually a more subtle, more poetic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:19.260 --> 00:38:24.540
more beautiful thing. Tim, how do you break down&nbsp;
the barrier between yourself and the people with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:29.940
whom you find yourself living as a guest? And&nbsp;
it's never bravado or macho. It's actually always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:29.940 --> 00:38:37.020
love and empathy and letting people know that you&nbsp;
believe they're somebody. And it seems so simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:37.020 --> 00:38:42.000
but you'd be astonished how many people&nbsp;
I encountered, particularly in Haiti,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:51.120
including Dr. Kline, who had no way whatsoever of&nbsp;
hanging out with those folks, just couldn't do it.&nbsp;

00:38:51.120 --> 00:38:56.880
And that's what I've been doing all my life. But&nbsp;
the important thing is, from the Haitian point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:56.880 --> 00:39:02.520
of view, the poison is not what makes a zombie.&nbsp;
And that's really important and that explains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:02.520 --> 00:39:09.300
how I got the formula so quickly. But the funny&nbsp;
part of this story is that it was Easter Sunday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:09.300 --> 00:39:16.620
when I returned to the United States through JFK&nbsp;
airport, and I had this suitcase made of surplus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.620 --> 00:39:23.820
7UP tin cans that was filled to the gunnels with&nbsp;
human bones, all the ingredients in various forms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.820 --> 00:39:29.520
I had a live Bufo marinus toad in my backpack,&nbsp;
the biggest toad in the world, 10 inches across,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:32.328
and I had no permits. And you couldn't do —
Tim Ferriss: Customs dream.&nbsp;

00:39:32.328 --> 00:39:37.680
Wade Davis: Yeah. You couldn't do this post&nbsp;
9/11. But I went up to this customs agent and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:37.680 --> 00:39:41.700
I just said, "Well, let's just see what he says."&nbsp;
So I opened up this thing, so only he could see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.700 --> 00:39:47.220
it. And he slammed it shut, Tim. And I'm not&nbsp;
going to give you exactly what he said because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:47.220 --> 00:39:51.480
they are children listening to your podcast.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, no. This doesn't need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:51.480 --> 00:39:55.740
family friendly. What did he say? You can tell us.
Wade Davis: He literally said in a New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:55.740 --> 00:40:02.160
accent, "Look, it's Easter fucking Sunday. I&nbsp;
didn't even want to work fucking work today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:08.640
I don't know who the fuck you are. Just get the&nbsp;
fuck out of here." And that's how the zombie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:10.980
poison came into America.
Tim Ferriss: Wow. Thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:10.980 --> 00:40:13.620
God for Easter Sunday and that guy.
Wade Davis: But then here's where we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:13.620 --> 00:40:20.100
get to your TTX. I analyzed the plants, took the&nbsp;
reptiles to the herpatologist and all the various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:20.100 --> 00:40:24.840
creatures to the various specialists. And I&nbsp;
finally got around to the fish. And I went to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:24.840 --> 00:40:31.020
the ichthyologist in the basement of the Museum&nbsp;
of Comparative Zoology. And this was like out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:31.020 --> 00:40:36.360
a movie. And I say to this wonderful character,&nbsp;
"Did you find anything in those fish?" And he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:36.360 --> 00:40:42.960
had his head inside a white shark. And as he heard&nbsp;
me, he bounced his head against the teeth, pulled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:48.000
his head out and said, "I thought you were the&nbsp;
poison people" because our museum was the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:53.760
center for the study of medicinal toxic plants and&nbsp;
hallucinogens, and then he goes to the shelf and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:53.760 --> 00:40:59.940
he doesn't pull out The Journal of Ichthyology,&nbsp;
he pulls out a pocketbook dimestore novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:59.940 --> 00:41:05.460
and it turns out to be written by Ian Fleming.
And it was either From Russia with Love or from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.460 --> 00:41:11.880
Dr. No. And at the one end of those two books, 007&nbsp;
gets kicked in the shin by the bad guy and dies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.880 --> 00:41:18.360
and he comes back to life in the next book because&nbsp;
he's been kicked with the poison tetrodotoxin. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:18.360 --> 00:41:23.100
that is what blew open the zombie study.
Tim Ferriss: TTX. Tetrodotoxin.&nbsp;

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:29.340
Wade Davis: Right. Because tetrodotoxin is a big&nbsp;
molecule that selectively blocks sodium channels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:29.340 --> 00:41:35.760
and brings on peripheral paralysis, dramatically&nbsp;
low metabolic rates, and consciousness is retained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:41.400
until death. And when you looked at the symptoms&nbsp;
of Narcisse, they match perfectly the symptoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:41.400 --> 00:41:49.200
of victims of fugu fish poisoning in Japan. The&nbsp;
fugu fish is a culinary delicacy. The chef must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.200 --> 00:41:55.260
eliminate most of the TTX, but not all, because&nbsp;
he wants the connoisseur to enjoy the pleasant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:55.260 --> 00:42:01.560
after effects of a mild intoxication. But because&nbsp;
some people screw up, lots of people have died.&nbsp;

00:42:01.560 --> 00:42:08.460
And there was a whole literature in Japan and&nbsp;
in the public, in newspapers, case after case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:08.460 --> 00:42:14.640
after case, of people nailed into their coffins&nbsp;
by mistake. So this changed everything. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.640 --> 00:42:21.000
suggested without doubt that the sorcerers in&nbsp;
Haiti had found, in their environment, a natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:27.720
product that not only could make someone appear to&nbsp;
be dead, but had done so many times in the past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:27.720 --> 00:42:33.780
So then you had to ask, what really is a zombie?&nbsp;
Who's controlling the process? And the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:33.780 --> 00:42:40.560
I was able to become the first person from outside&nbsp;
of Haiti ever to be initiated into the Bizango,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:40.560 --> 00:42:47.400
Sanpwèl, the secret societies that produced&nbsp;
the Tonton Macoutes, and I was able to at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:54.840
suggest that zombification was a form of ultimate&nbsp;
social sanction in which the individual lost their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.840 --> 00:43:01.560
personal autonomy and their physical freedom and&nbsp;
became cast into a state of purgatory that was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:01.560 --> 00:43:05.760
in a sense, worse than death.
So just before we leave zombies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:12.900
the whole purpose of this was, let's define the&nbsp;
drugs used to make zombies because no drug can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:18.780
make a social phenomena, but rather to take a&nbsp;
phenomena that had been used in an explicitly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:24.720
racist way, to denigrate an entire culture and&nbsp;
its religion, and to try to make sense out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:24.720 --> 00:43:31.560
sensation. And so if the scientists sent me to&nbsp;
Haiti to find the chemicals used to make zombies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:31.560 --> 00:43:39.540
I found myself instead studying the psychological,&nbsp;
social, historical, political dimensions of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.540 --> 00:43:46.080
chemical possibility. And that's what made&nbsp;
the research so exciting. The pursuit of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:46.080 --> 00:43:53.640
little preparation opened up these historic and&nbsp;
ethnographic vistas that no one had seen before.&nbsp;

00:43:53.640 --> 00:43:58.500
Tim Ferriss: I have two questions related to&nbsp;
this, and I'm sure we could have dozens more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:58.500 --> 00:44:02.040
but there are so many other things I'd love to&nbsp;
chat about. But two follow-up questions. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:02.040 --> 00:44:09.840
first is, what role, if any, does the Datura play&nbsp;
in this entire process? And there are documented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:09.840 --> 00:44:15.300
deaths every year, in the United States at least,&nbsp;
related to people who attempt to DIY some type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:16.080 --> 00:44:23.700
trip from Datura. So the role of Datura, if any.&nbsp;
And then secondly, and this is based on a somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:29.220
fragmented recollection of watching some type&nbsp;
of news program that was purportedly covering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:29.220 --> 00:44:37.140
this social phenomenon of zombification.&nbsp;
And my impression from that was that some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:37.140 --> 00:44:44.580
people remain in servitude for other people as&nbsp;
zombies for an extended period of time. And I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.580 --> 00:44:50.322
wondering if there is — what are the primary&nbsp;
contributing factors to a situation like that?&nbsp;

00:44:50.322 --> 00:44:54.000
Wade Davis: Well, one of the things you always&nbsp;
have to do in this kind of research is separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:58.440
what we might call the emic from the etic, which&nbsp;
are unnecessarily technical terms in anthropology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:58.440 --> 00:45:03.540
the view from within, the view from&nbsp;
without. Why don't the people in India eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:10.080
cows? Well, it's prohibited by the scriptures,&nbsp;
but also they need the oxen to work the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:10.080 --> 00:45:16.200
fields. Those would be the two points of view.
First, let me answer your question about Datura.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:16.200 --> 00:45:21.540
Datura is in the solanaceae, the potato family,&nbsp;
the family of choice of black magicians around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:21.540 --> 00:45:29.460
world. The tree Daturas, the brugmansias in South&nbsp;
America, are known as the jaguar's intoxicant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:38.640
the tree of the evil eagle. And these plants have&nbsp;
in them powerful tropane alkaloids, scopolamine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:38.640 --> 00:45:44.940
and atropine in particular, that induce a state&nbsp;
of psychotic delirium, visions of hellfire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:54.480
a burning thirst, amnesia, a sensation of flight.&nbsp;
These are incredibly dangerous and horrific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:55.140 --> 00:46:00.300
plants that the shaman in the Andes&nbsp;
take only if everything else fails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:00.300 --> 00:46:06.060
almost with the idea that just in touching the&nbsp;
realm of madness, they might achieve illumination.&nbsp;

00:46:06.060 --> 00:46:13.620
And what's interesting, and going back to&nbsp;
the victims of tetrodotoxication in Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:13.620 --> 00:46:19.140
if you eat fugu fish and you get poisoned&nbsp;
and you're put into your coffin and you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:19.140 --> 00:46:25.080
lucky enough to be rescued, you come out of the&nbsp;
coffin and you say, "Oh, that's terrible. What a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:25.080 --> 00:46:30.360
mistake. I'll never eat fugu again." But that's&nbsp;
the end of it, right? But remember, the Haitian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:31.200 --> 00:46:37.380
doesn't sit around questioning whether zombies&nbsp;
exist. He or she knows in the fiber of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:37.380 --> 00:46:44.400
being that they do and or she knows why&nbsp;
a zombie is made, a form of punishment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.060 --> 00:46:54.240
within the traditional culture. And so we don't&nbsp;
know exactly what might or might not occur.&nbsp;

00:46:54.240 --> 00:47:01.020
We know that tetrodotoxin reaches a crisis in&nbsp;
about six hours. And if you survive that crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:01.020 --> 00:47:08.340
you have no physiological damage whatsoever. But&nbsp;
of course, in the case of the Haitian zombie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:08.340 --> 00:47:15.600
whether the individual is symbolically put&nbsp;
into the ground, placed behind a shade or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:23.100
hidden from view, whatever, when they come out of&nbsp;
the tetrodotoxication, they know what's happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:23.100 --> 00:47:30.960
to them and they're in a state of incredible&nbsp;
suggestibility and fear. And what the Datura&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:37.260
may serve, it's known as the konkonm zonbi,&nbsp;
the zombie's cucumber. And I was at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:37.260 --> 00:47:45.000
told by many informants that at that point of&nbsp;
disorientation, the victim is given Datura, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:45.000 --> 00:47:51.780
must be an extraordinary, horrific experience,&nbsp;
and one that would sort of seal the psychological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:53.280 --> 00:47:58.560
conviction that he or she had, in&nbsp;
fact, been punished in this way.&nbsp;

00:47:58.560 --> 00:48:05.640
Now, when you mentioned the idea of slavery,&nbsp;
well, there's no incentive to create, in Haiti,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:05.640 --> 00:48:10.560
a force of indentured labor. But again,&nbsp;
critically given the colonial history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:11.820 --> 00:48:19.860
slavery implies a destiny almost worse than life&nbsp;
itself. And by the same token, I mentioned earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:19.860 --> 00:48:27.000
that they don't believe the poison creates a&nbsp;
zombie. To make a zombie, I have to capture, Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:27.000 --> 00:48:32.280
your little good angel, your soul, the soul that&nbsp;
makes you Tim Ferriss as opposed to the soul that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:32.280 --> 00:48:37.500
makes me Wade Davis, not the soul that we both&nbsp;
share, that all sentient beings share, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:38.040 --> 00:48:44.760
soul that creates your personality. That's why&nbsp;
a zombie appears comatose. The fear in Haiti is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:44.760 --> 00:48:51.000
not of zombies, it's of becoming a zombie. And&nbsp;
so I once asked a Vodou priest, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:51.000 --> 00:48:56.940
if it was just a matter of returning the soul to&nbsp;
the victim, could that be done and the person made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:56.940 --> 00:49:03.120
whole? And the man who I asked that question&nbsp;
was the great emperor of the secret societies.&nbsp;

00:49:03.120 --> 00:49:07.140
He had been head of the Tonton Macoutes for&nbsp;
a fifth of the country. I once asked Herard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:07.140 --> 00:49:11.340
if during the revolution he had ever killed&nbsp;
anybody. He said, "I never killed any people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:11.340 --> 00:49:17.160
just enemies." And so I asked him, "Couldn't&nbsp;
you just give the soul back to the person?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:17.160 --> 00:49:21.660
And he said, "Yeah, you could do that. But&nbsp;
on the other hand, blan, if you were a woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:21.660 --> 00:49:25.980
would you want an ex-zombie to ask you to&nbsp;
dance?" And of course, what he was getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:25.980 --> 00:49:31.140
at is that a zombie becomes a total pariah.
Now, remember what I said about Narcisse when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:31.140 --> 00:49:36.780
he first went back to his village. Nobody doubted&nbsp;
that it was the long-lost, presumed-to-be-dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:39.888
brother. But did they welcome him with open arms?
Tim Ferriss: No.&nbsp;

00:49:39.888 --> 00:49:44.580
Wade Davis: No. They told him, "Get the&nbsp;
hell out of here." Because he had died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:44.580 --> 00:49:49.560
socially. He had died spiritually. They&nbsp;
wanted nothing to do with him. And that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:49.560 --> 00:49:54.420
really, is what a zombie is all about.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, thank you for that. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:54.420 --> 00:50:01.080
part of the reason I was asking about the Datura,&nbsp;
as I do have some familiarity with brugmansia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:01.080 --> 00:50:08.820
and have had some exposure to the Awajún or the&nbsp;
Aguarunas who use it pretty extensively for not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:08.820 --> 00:50:16.680
just dark purposes or power purposes, but for many&nbsp;
different conditions. But my understanding is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:16.680 --> 00:50:22.920
also organized crime, I want to say in Colombia,&nbsp;
for a period of time, was using brugmansia seeds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:22.920 --> 00:50:28.740
which I think they're called burundanga. They&nbsp;
would pulverize and someone with a map would walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:28.740 --> 00:50:33.720
up to a mark and say, "Could you tell me where&nbsp;
this place is?" blow the powder into their face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:33.720 --> 00:50:39.900
at which point that person would become highly&nbsp;
suggestible and also have developed amnesia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:39.900 --> 00:50:44.100
So you could, say, take someone back to their own&nbsp;
apartment, ask them to help load their things into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:44.100 --> 00:50:48.000
a truck. They would have no recollection of this&nbsp;
even though they would be coherent interacting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:48.000 --> 00:50:54.900
with security guards. And for that reason, I was&nbsp;
wondering if, perhaps, the Datura was used in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:55.560 --> 00:50:59.880
similar fashion to increase suggestibility?
Wade Davis: You're absolutely right on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:59.880 --> 00:51:07.260
Tim. The word Datura, the name of the genus, comes&nbsp;
from ancient India. Bands of criminals known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:07.260 --> 00:51:12.588
the Daturas who used it as a knockout drug.
Tim Ferriss: Wow. I did not know that. Wow.&nbsp;

00:51:12.588 --> 00:51:18.780
Wade Davis: I was once in San Agustín with a&nbsp;
Colombian and this Australian kind of hippie guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:18.780 --> 00:51:24.840
this is back in the early '70s, and he spoke&nbsp;
about how he ate a bunch of tree Datura in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:24.840 --> 00:51:31.860
hotel on the coast and ended up walking around&nbsp;
naked, the Barranquilla, a public market for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:31.860 --> 00:51:36.780
five days before he was finally arrested. And at&nbsp;
our table, Tim, there was a wonderful Colombian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:36.780 --> 00:51:41.700
hippie girl who looked up at me and said, "I know&nbsp;
that market. I wouldn't even buy a mango there."&nbsp;

00:51:41.700 --> 00:51:50.340
But I'll tell you in my book, Magdalena, about&nbsp;
the Great River of Colombia, there's a story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:54.840
my good friend William Vargas, who was on&nbsp;
his way to university. And these stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:54.840 --> 00:52:01.440
like you recounted, are part of travelers' lore&nbsp;
in Colombia. But I had never met anyone who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:01.440 --> 00:52:07.320
actually endured this. And someone on a bus&nbsp;
offered him a cake or a cookie, which he ate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:07.320 --> 00:52:13.980
And that was the last thing he remembered. And&nbsp;
he came to four days later in a kind of psychic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:13.980 --> 00:52:22.440
horror, having lost everything that he owned and&nbsp;
had his entire psychological state shattered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:22.440 --> 00:52:28.680
So these are very powerful drugs indeed.
Tim Ferriss: So I guess there are a few lessons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:28.680 --> 00:52:34.320
Number one, don't take or accept candy from&nbsp;
strangers, everybody listening. And do not play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:34.320 --> 00:52:40.380
around with these plants and molecules. They are&nbsp;
no joke and can do a tremendous amount of damage.&nbsp;

00:52:40.380 --> 00:52:46.245
So I would like to rewind the clock a bit.&nbsp;
And you mentioned Richard Evans Schultes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:46.800 --> 00:52:50.640
I've had a number of discussions about him&nbsp;
on the podcast before, so he may come up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:50.640 --> 00:52:55.800
but I would actually like to invoke a&nbsp;
different name, which is David Maybury-Lewis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:55.800 --> 00:53:03.540
if I'm pronouncing that correctly. Could you&nbsp;
please describe who he was and what you learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:03.540 --> 00:53:14.460
from him or what lessons he imparted to you?
Wade Davis: I was so incredibly fortunate, looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:14.460 --> 00:53:22.320
back. I was an undergraduate at Harvard. I got my&nbsp;
PhD at Harvard, but I began as an anthropologist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:22.320 --> 00:53:30.540
And David Maybury-Lewis was my undergraduate&nbsp;
tutor. He was one of the great Americanists. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:30.540 --> 00:53:41.340
had traveled into the heart of Brazil in the 1950s&nbsp;
to live amongst the Xavante and, before that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:41.340 --> 00:53:48.420
the Xerente, who at the time were said to be&nbsp;
the most feared indigenous groups in Brazil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:49.320 --> 00:53:56.760
And he was a great humanist. And while I was with&nbsp;
him, he created Cultural Survival with his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:56.760 --> 00:54:03.600
Pia. And he absolutely lived in a way, although&nbsp;
he wasn't really a Boasian because he was from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:03.600 --> 00:54:11.460
the British tradition of social anthropology.&nbsp;
But he absolutely believed that activism was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:11.460 --> 00:54:19.800
integral part of the anthropological endeavor.&nbsp;
When you have languages disappearing, when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:19.800 --> 00:54:28.980
have indigenous people suffering the predations&nbsp;
of the rubber era, there's a moral obligation to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:28.980 --> 00:54:36.780
both tell their stories and to work with them,&nbsp;
I think, as liaison, a conduit to the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:37.620 --> 00:54:41.700
facilitating or amplifying their voices,&nbsp;
bringing their concerns to the world.&nbsp;

00:54:41.700 --> 00:54:48.060
And that was something that I had in the fiber&nbsp;
of my DNA because of my association with David.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:48.060 --> 00:54:54.120
I was also very fortunate, as you mentioned,&nbsp;
to fall into the orbit of Professor Schultes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:54.120 --> 00:54:59.940
But Professor Schultes was a man of action&nbsp;
and deed. In 18 years of studying with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:59.940 --> 00:55:04.080
I don't think I ever had an intellectual&nbsp;
conversation. He would say things to you like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:04.080 --> 00:55:09.300
"There's one river I'd like you to know," knowing&nbsp;
full well that the process of getting to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:09.300 --> 00:55:14.820
river would involve experiences guaranteed&nbsp;
to assure you that if you emerge out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:14.820 --> 00:55:20.640
forest of that confluence alive, you'd be a wiser&nbsp;
and a more complete human being. But Schultes was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:20.640 --> 00:55:28.080
not a man of ideas. He was a botanist. That's&nbsp;
what he was, a plant explorer. And I loved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:28.080 --> 00:55:34.740
botanical exploration largely because it provided&nbsp;
the conduit to culture. If you want to live with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:34.740 --> 00:55:40.380
the Inuit in the high Arctic, you better become&nbsp;
a hunter, because that is a measure of a man.&nbsp;

00:55:40.380 --> 00:55:48.720
If you want to engage the priests in Haiti,&nbsp;
you have to serve the Loa. You've got to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:48.720 --> 00:55:55.320
part of the circle of Vodou. Otherwise, what&nbsp;
are you doing? And of course, in the Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:55.320 --> 00:56:01.500
the plants become the perfect conduit to culture.&nbsp;
You're not turning up at some maloca and saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:01.500 --> 00:56:06.180
"I'm here to study your sex life." If someone&nbsp;
turned up at our doorstep like that, we'd call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:06.180 --> 00:56:11.460
the police. But studying the plants makes so&nbsp;
much sense to those for whom the plants are so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:11.460 --> 00:56:19.620
important. At the same time, most ethnobotanists&nbsp;
of my generation were notoriously uneducated when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:19.620 --> 00:56:26.160
it came to the nuances of anthropology and&nbsp;
ethnography. And I was very, very fortunate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:27.120 --> 00:56:34.440
in having David, a mentor who carried all the way&nbsp;
through graduate school. I taught more courses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:34.440 --> 00:56:42.720
for David than I did for Schultes. And in fact,&nbsp;
my ideas that in the wake of all my botanical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:42.720 --> 00:56:48.660
research, I actually kind of discovered in the&nbsp;
wake of the Haiti work, that what I was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:48.660 --> 00:56:55.680
interested in was culture as opposed to plants.
I always still use plants to inform much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:55.680 --> 00:57:02.280
of my writing, but it was the ethno part of&nbsp;
ethnobotany that intrigued me. And in that sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:03.120 --> 00:57:09.360
all of my ideas that I have been exploring&nbsp;
through the 13 years at the Geographic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:09.360 --> 00:57:13.440
the decade as a professor of anthropology&nbsp;
at the University of British Columbia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:14.340 --> 00:57:19.560
everything traces back to David.
And on the subject of mentors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:19.560 --> 00:57:27.600
I think this is so important for young people&nbsp;
listening to this broadcast. I grew up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:27.600 --> 00:57:34.800
the simplest of middle class homes. My father's&nbsp;
spirit, in many ways, had been broken in the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:35.340 --> 00:57:42.300
as had my mother in a different set of&nbsp;
circumstances. There was a lot of love, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:42.300 --> 00:57:51.540
not a lot of activity, creativity. And it was very&nbsp;
clear to me that I had to get out. And I began,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:52.620 --> 00:57:57.960
a very young age, jumping off cliffs.
And as Terence McKenna always said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:57.960 --> 00:58:03.000
"The great lesson of life is that when&nbsp;
you do that, you don't land on rock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:03.000 --> 00:58:08.340
you land on a feather bed. The world exists to&nbsp;
lift you up, not beat you down." Jim Whittaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:08.340 --> 00:58:13.920
the great climber, good friend of mine, said that&nbsp;
"If you're not living on the edge when young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:13.920 --> 00:58:20.580
you're taking up too much space." But what I&nbsp;
found myself having to do, Tim, was fling myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:20.580 --> 00:58:26.640
into the arms of mentors. And those mentors&nbsp;
could be an old Gitxsan elder who I recorded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:26.640 --> 00:58:33.480
mythology with and hunted with for 40 years.&nbsp;
It could be an engineer who taught me how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:35.160 --> 00:58:41.700
understand the complexities of industrial logging&nbsp;
when I spent a year in the bush. I've always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:41.700 --> 00:58:48.060
believed that nothing is beneath you. Nothing is a&nbsp;
waste of time unless you make it so. A cab driver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:48.060 --> 00:58:54.120
can have as much to teach you as a professor at&nbsp;
university if you're open to the possibility.&nbsp;

00:58:54.120 --> 00:59:00.900
And I always found that if I just gave&nbsp;
myself fully to these mentors like Schultes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:00.900 --> 00:59:08.040
like David Maybury-Lewis, like Dr. Kline, and many&nbsp;
that I've had the privilege to engage since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:09.240 --> 00:59:15.360
I was able to become the most important thing,&nbsp;
which is the architect of my own life. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:15.360 --> 00:59:22.020
is what I say to young people, "Be patient,&nbsp;
never compromise, give your destiny time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:22.020 --> 00:59:29.580
find you. Bitterness always comes to those who&nbsp;
look back on a life of choices imposed upon them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:29.580 --> 00:59:34.440
from the outside. And you may not make all the&nbsp;
right decisions, but if you own those decisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:34.440 --> 00:59:40.920
they all become the right ones because together&nbsp;
they become the path of your own creation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:40.920 --> 00:59:46.260
you become the architect of your own life."
And that is something so very important. And in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:46.260 --> 00:59:54.000
that spirit, Tim, I try to do everything I can to&nbsp;
help young people. I answer every email and I get,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:54.000 --> 01:00:00.780
as you do, thousands of emails from young people.&nbsp;
And very often what they're saying, they may&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:00.780 --> 01:00:06.600
have a specific question, but what they're really&nbsp;
saying is not just, "How can I be you?" They know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:06.600 --> 01:00:12.540
they can't be me. But what they really want to&nbsp;
know is, "How can I live a life of authenticity?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:12.540 --> 01:00:19.980
How can I live a life where I'm not strapped to&nbsp;
a laptop at a desk and a cubicle? How can I find&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:19.980 --> 01:00:29.400
a way to monetize the creativity of my own life?&nbsp;
How can I make myself, and the act of being alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:29.400 --> 01:00:36.420
my vocation, recognizing that any job one has is&nbsp;
just a passing thing, a kind of filter through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:36.420 --> 01:00:41.580
which to see the world and only for a time?"
And the real challenge is to make the art of life&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:41.580 --> 01:00:47.880
itself your vocation. And I always answer those&nbsp;
because if you don't answer, it's not a neutral&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:48.660 --> 01:00:54.480
gesture. It's a slap in the wrist. They're&nbsp;
reaching out to you and all you have to say is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:54.480 --> 01:01:02.400
"Wonderful idea, Charlie. Go for it. Your friend."&nbsp;
And that takes about as much time as deleting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:02.400 --> 01:01:06.660
message. And I learned that from Schultes.
I'll tell you one wonderful story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:08.820 --> 01:01:13.680
The most famous botanical collections of&nbsp;
Schultes, and Mark would certainly confirm this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:13.680 --> 01:01:21.360
were between 1950 and 1953 when free of the&nbsp;
rubber emergency, he was free to collect medicinal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:21.360 --> 01:01:28.740
plants. He described the use of 2,000 medicinal&nbsp;
plants previously unknown to science. And with him&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:28.740 --> 01:01:34.560
on all those collections was a man called Isidoro&nbsp;
Cabrera. Now, when you do botany and you collect&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:34.560 --> 01:01:40.140
plants, the senior botanist's name goes first.&nbsp;
So Tim Plowman and Wade Davis, Plowman and Davis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:40.140 --> 01:01:47.400
and that's how you do it on the specimens; it's&nbsp;
kind of a formal thing. But you never are said&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:47.400 --> 01:01:53.700
to put an Indigenous helper on the label as&nbsp;
if he's an equal to you, but Schultes did.&nbsp;

01:01:54.780 --> 01:02:01.140
He met Isidoro when Isidoro's farm had been&nbsp;
burned in the war. His parents had been murdered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:01.140 --> 01:02:09.900
he had no food, he was absolutely nothing. And&nbsp;
he ended his life a full professor of botany with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:09.900 --> 01:02:16.260
multiple honorary degrees. And before he died,&nbsp;
when I was writing the book One River, I went&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:16.260 --> 01:02:24.300
to see Isidoro in Cali. And I said, "Professor,&nbsp;
I want you to think really carefully. I want you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:24.300 --> 01:02:31.920
to remember the first moment you met Professor&nbsp;
Schultes in the forest, in Macarena. What was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:31.920 --> 01:02:39.060
it like? What did he say?" And he looked at me&nbsp;
very pensively, and then suddenly, there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:39.060 --> 01:02:45.947
twinkle in his eye, and he said, "He looked at me&nbsp;
like I was somebody." Isn't that beautiful, Tim?&nbsp;

01:02:45.947 --> 01:02:48.720
Tim Ferriss: That is beautiful.
Wade Davis: And in class-riddled&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:48.720 --> 01:02:56.040
Colombia at that time, for a Harvard professor&nbsp;
to do that, and it made that young man's life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:56.040 --> 01:03:00.660
Pulled him out of misery, gave him a&nbsp;
career and a great gift to Colombia.&nbsp;

01:03:00.660 --> 01:03:07.500
Tim Ferriss: What a story. And I&nbsp;
want to come back to, I suppose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:09.120 --> 01:03:15.240
frames and lenses for a moment, and also Jim&nbsp;
Whittaker. So for those who don't know, the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:15.240 --> 01:03:24.000
American to summit Everest, if my research is not&nbsp;
lying to me. And I'm looking at an excerpt from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:25.140 --> 01:03:33.180
alumni stories on the brentwood.bc.ca website,&nbsp;
and he comes up. And there's a line that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:33.180 --> 01:03:38.220
would like to explore because I think it's Maya&nbsp;
Angelou, if I'm pronouncing her name correctly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:38.220 --> 01:03:44.580
said that courage is the mother virtue that&nbsp;
unlocks all other virtues. Because effectively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:44.580 --> 01:03:47.460
I'm paraphrasing here, but at the&nbsp;
breaking point, you need courage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:47.460 --> 01:03:52.500
to enact or to enable those other virtues.
And there's a line here, and I don't know if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:52.500 --> 01:03:56.640
it is Jim's or yours, but either way,&nbsp;
I would love for you to expand on it.&nbsp;

01:03:56.640 --> 01:04:00.720
"Pessimism is an indulgence,&nbsp;
orthodoxy is the enemy of invention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:00.720 --> 01:04:04.920
despair an insult to the imagination."
And I want to bring this up because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:06.180 --> 01:04:10.980
it strikes me that a lot of people, not&nbsp;
just very young people, but many people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:10.980 --> 01:04:18.360
overall feel a certain psycho-emotional malaise&nbsp;
right now, a sense of overwhelm that has led to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:19.440 --> 01:04:25.200
pessimism or nihilism. And so it seems&nbsp;
to me that optimism is the unlock here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:25.200 --> 01:04:29.808
So could you elaborate on the "pessimism&nbsp;
is an indulgence" and so on in that line?&nbsp;

01:04:29.808 --> 01:04:35.160
Wade Davis: Yeah, that was actually my&nbsp;
line, not Jim's. People are always asking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:35.160 --> 01:04:38.820
we're always asking each other, "Are you&nbsp;
optimistic?" And I kind of feel like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:39.780 --> 01:04:44.760
how can you not be optimistic? I mean, that's the&nbsp;
purpose of life itself. And if you're a father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:44.760 --> 01:04:53.280
you absolutely have an obligation to remain&nbsp;
hopeful. And given how many gifts we have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:54.900 --> 01:05:02.100
surely pessimism does become something of an&nbsp;
indulgence. We're all so caught in the present&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:02.100 --> 01:05:07.200
these days, so little sense of history,&nbsp;
and we forget how much we've achieved.&nbsp;

01:05:07.980 --> 01:05:13.800
But when you think about it, Tim, in my lifetime,&nbsp;
women have gone from the kitchen to the boardroom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:13.800 --> 01:05:19.740
People of color, from the woodshed to the&nbsp;
White House. Gay people, men and women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:19.740 --> 01:05:26.100
from the closet to the altar. When we think&nbsp;
of the environment, when I was a young kid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:26.100 --> 01:05:31.740
just getting people to stop throwing garbage out&nbsp;
of a car window was a great environmental victory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:31.740 --> 01:05:39.780
Nobody spoke about the biosphere or biodiversity.&nbsp;
Now these are terms familiar to schoolchildren. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:39.780 --> 01:05:47.460
what's not to love about a world capable of such&nbsp;
social transformation, such scientific genius?&nbsp;

01:05:48.000 --> 01:05:58.800
Just think about that moment on Christmas Eve,&nbsp;
1968 when Apollo went around the dark side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:58.800 --> 01:06:04.980
the moon and emerged to see, for the first time&nbsp;
in human history, not a sunrise or a moonrise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:04.980 --> 01:06:10.560
but an Earthrise. And in that incredible&nbsp;
moment, we suddenly saw the Earth as it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:10.560 --> 01:06:19.740
not this infinite horizon, but a fragile blue&nbsp;
planet, as the astronauts famously reported,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:19.740 --> 01:06:25.020
floating in the velvet void of space. And I&nbsp;
think everything has changed with that. Like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:25.020 --> 01:06:32.160
a flash of illumination, it swept over the&nbsp;
world. We never will think again about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:32.160 --> 01:06:37.800
natural world in the same way we did before that&nbsp;
vision. And even today, as I mentioned earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:37.800 --> 01:06:44.160
I think, the revelations of genetics, showing&nbsp;
us indisputably that race is a total fiction.&nbsp;

01:06:44.160 --> 01:06:51.060
Well, that hasn't really gotten into&nbsp;
the zeitgeist yet as the moonshot has,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:51.060 --> 01:06:58.200
but it will. And I think that we're living&nbsp;
through extraordinarily exciting times&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:59.820 --> 01:07:05.640
and extraordinarily challenging times. But as&nbsp;
I say to all young people, "What generation has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:05.640 --> 01:07:12.720
ever come of age in a world at peace, a world&nbsp;
without troubles?" It's very interesting. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:12.720 --> 01:07:19.500
of the ways I, Tim, keep my optimism, my dad&nbsp;
wasn't a religious man. His spirit was broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:19.500 --> 01:07:24.900
in the war. I never saw the inside of a church in&nbsp;
his presence. But he did believe in good and evil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:24.900 --> 01:07:30.660
And he used to say to me, "There's good and evil&nbsp;
in the world. Take your pick and get on with it."&nbsp;

01:07:30.660 --> 01:07:35.880
And it was incredibly wise, because we have&nbsp;
this sort of thing in the Christian tradition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:35.880 --> 01:07:41.100
particularly, that if we just wait long enough,&nbsp;
good's going to triumph over evil and we'll all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:41.100 --> 01:07:48.420
somehow be dissolved in the Rapture. Well, ain't&nbsp;
going to happen. And famously, in Medieval times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:48.420 --> 01:07:53.460
if you asked the obvious question, "If God's all&nbsp;
powerful, why does he allow evil to exist?" you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:53.460 --> 01:07:57.960
were burned at the stake for heresy. But in&nbsp;
the Indian tradition, the Vedic tradition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:57.960 --> 01:08:03.060
by contrast, when Lord Krishna was asked&nbsp;
that very question, "If God's all powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:03.060 --> 01:08:09.600
why does he allow evil to exist in the universe?"&nbsp;
Lord Krishna said to the disciple, "To thicken&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:09.600 --> 01:08:14.520
the plot." In other words, good and evil walk&nbsp;
hand in hand. You're never going to lose one.&nbsp;

01:08:14.520 --> 01:08:20.760
You've got to take your side. And the&nbsp;
purpose of life is not to triumph over evil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:20.760 --> 01:08:27.900
but keep pushing the wheel of justice forward.&nbsp;
And when you realize that that is the end point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:27.900 --> 01:08:34.260
you then never expect to win. And if you&nbsp;
never expect to win, you're not disappointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:34.260 --> 01:08:39.420
when you lose. And because of that, you&nbsp;
can keep fighting with the same idealism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:39.420 --> 01:08:47.040
the same energy when you're 69 years old, as&nbsp;
I am today, that I had when I was 20 years old&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:47.040 --> 01:08:52.980
and marching against the war in Vietnam.
Tim Ferriss: I'd like to discuss rites&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:52.980 --> 01:08:59.460
of passage. And specifically, we don't have to&nbsp;
necessarily focus on this, but this is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:59.460 --> 01:09:06.480
that often ends up on my radar of consciousness&nbsp;
because I have many males in my audience. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:06.480 --> 01:09:13.620
there seems to be a distinct lack of rites of&nbsp;
passage for men in most westernized societies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:13.620 --> 01:09:20.040
or many westernized societies. And I would love&nbsp;
for you to describe a chapter in your life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:20.040 --> 01:09:26.520
and I'm most certainly going to butcher this&nbsp;
pronunciation, but Spatsizi. Did I get that right?&nbsp;

01:09:26.520 --> 01:09:32.589
Wade Davis: Yeah, Spatsizi. Red Goat.
Tim Ferriss: So could you explain what that is?&nbsp;

01:09:32.589 --> 01:09:36.240
Wade Davis: Before I jump into my, perhaps, story,&nbsp;
rites of passage exist all around the world for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:36.240 --> 01:09:43.740
a very specific reason. It's not a coincidence&nbsp;
or an accident that they involve pain, whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:43.740 --> 01:09:49.920
it's scarification, whether it's the severing of&nbsp;
the foreskin, whether it's the pain of ordeal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:51.540 --> 01:09:56.580
the ingestion — I mean, for example, the&nbsp;
Algonquins, speaking of Datura, their initiation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:56.580 --> 01:10:03.720
rite was to put the young lads in the long house,&nbsp;
seal it shut, and make them eat Datura for two&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:03.720 --> 01:10:09.180
weeks, so that they would forget what it was&nbsp;
to become boys, to learn what it was to be men.&nbsp;

01:10:09.180 --> 01:10:15.240
But the reason all these ordeals that you know so&nbsp;
much about, vision quests, et cetera, have pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:15.240 --> 01:10:22.380
is because a message has to be clear: this is the&nbsp;
end. It's not about the twiddling of thumbs. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:22.380 --> 01:10:30.960
are passing on to you the obligation of adulthood.&nbsp;
You now hold the destiny of our people in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:30.960 --> 01:10:39.360
hands. This is not trivial. You best be prepared.&nbsp;
And I think whether it's with women who go through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:39.360 --> 01:10:46.260
their own rituals, which are always sort of&nbsp;
timed to the first menses or the first period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:46.260 --> 01:10:55.740
fertility, transforming a girl into a woman,&nbsp;
a potential mother, or it's a boy proving his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:55.740 --> 01:11:03.660
manhood. Now this has become rather frowned&nbsp;
upon in our politically correct woke world.&nbsp;

01:11:03.660 --> 01:11:10.920
But the truth is, young men, I've never known&nbsp;
a young lad who didn't want that challenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:10.920 --> 01:11:19.800
It's that idea of proving oneselves, not in&nbsp;
a gratuitously macho way, but literally in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:19.800 --> 01:11:26.040
kind of organic way of grit and courage and&nbsp;
strength. And I think that's why. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:26.040 --> 01:11:31.980
for example, I've got very close friends in&nbsp;
the Navy SEALs, and they all have a kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:31.980 --> 01:11:37.140
calm confidence because they've been on what&nbsp;
Joseph Campbell called the Hero's Journey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:37.140 --> 01:11:44.340
And I think those of us, like myself, brought&nbsp;
up in a society that we did not have obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:44.340 --> 01:11:51.660
outlets, we created our own hero's journeys.
And for me, it was always either through work or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:51.660 --> 01:12:00.240
travel. You mentioned Spatsizi. Well, I was living&nbsp;
on Haida Gwaii in Northwest British Columbia in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:00.240 --> 01:12:07.860
the mid-'70s. And I was very much critical of&nbsp;
industrial clear-cut logging, but I felt that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:07.860 --> 01:12:14.280
I best learn something about it. So I lied about&nbsp;
my credentials and managed to hire on as a logging&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:14.280 --> 01:12:18.240
forestry engineer in one of the toughest logging&nbsp;
camps in the west coast of British Columbia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:18.240 --> 01:12:23.340
where I stayed a year. And I learned everything&nbsp;
about the business, including the corruption.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:23.340 --> 01:12:30.720
And it was a fantastic experience because I&nbsp;
also learned that the men and women fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:30.720 --> 01:12:35.880
off hunger with a chainsaw were not my enemy.
And I learned that in all of these conflicts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:35.880 --> 01:12:40.320
particularly around resources, there are&nbsp;
never any enemies, only solutions. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:40.320 --> 01:12:46.620
kind of escaped that camp, taking a job as the&nbsp;
first park ranger in what had just been created,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:46.620 --> 01:12:54.420
Canada's biggest roadless wilderness park. And my&nbsp;
job description was deliciously vague: wilderness&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:54.420 --> 01:13:00.540
assessment and public relations. In two four-month&nbsp;
seasons, I saw 12 people. There was no one to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:00.540 --> 01:13:09.960
relate publicly to. And these travels to South&nbsp;
America. And even in Haiti, you have to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:09.960 --> 01:13:15.660
that during the course of that research, it&nbsp;
turns out I never knew who was paying for it all.&nbsp;

01:13:15.660 --> 01:13:21.120
And I'll tell you, if you'd like, a story&nbsp;
of the night I had to light myself on fire.&nbsp;

01:13:21.840 --> 01:13:24.888
Tim Ferriss: Well, I can't say&nbsp;
no to that. So yes, please.&nbsp;

01:13:24.888 --> 01:13:30.000
Wade Davis: Well, remember I said I became very&nbsp;
close friends with Marcel Pierre, and we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:34.740
like brothers by the end of the many years I was&nbsp;
there. And his wife was dying of uterine cancer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:34.740 --> 01:13:39.420
and he was so sad. He came to me and I&nbsp;
bought all this blood for her and she&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:39.420 --> 01:13:44.940
still was dying. And I took him back to get&nbsp;
a tap-tap. And for once I was dressed like an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:44.940 --> 01:13:46.602
American tourist, and I didn't have —
Tim Ferriss: What is a tap-tap?&nbsp;

01:13:46.602 --> 01:13:52.920
Wade Davis: Oh, a tap-tap's a local bus in Haiti.&nbsp;
And I went in my little Jeep and I didn't have my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:53.520 --> 01:13:59.160
wallet or any money. And I got a flat tire&nbsp;
after I dropped him off. And I said to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:59.160 --> 01:14:03.000
guy at the side of the road who fixed tires&nbsp;
manually, I said, "Can you fix my tire?" He&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:03.000 --> 01:14:08.160
starts fixing my tire. Then I say in Creole, "Nez&nbsp;
me pa gen kòb" — "I've got no money." And then he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:08.160 --> 01:14:13.620
said, "Ki kalite Blan say ou?" and he started&nbsp;
hassling me. And I just wasn't in the mood. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:13.620 --> 01:14:18.960
should have not done this, but I took his hand&nbsp;
and I gave him the secret society handshake. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:18.960 --> 01:14:23.940
then he blanched back and then he said again,&nbsp;
"What kind of white are you?" And then we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:23.940 --> 01:14:28.890
a big laugh and he said, "Nou gen gwo lig aswè&nbsp;
a" — "We've got a big thing happening tonight."&nbsp;

01:14:28.890 --> 01:14:34.320
And I'd broken down, by chance, right by a Bizango&nbsp;
chanpwel — secret society temple. So I went back&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:34.320 --> 01:14:41.760
that night, for the first time unescorted by a&nbsp;
powerful Vodou priest. And the ceremony begins&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:41.760 --> 01:14:47.700
like any ceremony with the invocation of Legba,&nbsp;
the spirit of communication. And the dances are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:47.700 --> 01:14:52.020
just Vodou dances. But then at midnight&nbsp;
you hear the fouet kash, the whip crack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:52.020 --> 01:14:57.540
and the conch trumpet, the symbol of the&nbsp;
revolution, blow. And then the order goes out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:57.540 --> 01:15:02.580
"Soldiers of the night, change skins!" — "Sòlda&nbsp;
nan lannuit ! Chanje la po!" And everybody goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:02.580 --> 01:15:08.640
hundreds of people, go into this temple and&nbsp;
emerge in these anonymous black and red robes.&nbsp;

01:15:08.640 --> 01:15:15.060
And at that moment, six men came and grabbed me&nbsp;
and flung me into a chamber. And I rolled around&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:15.060 --> 01:15:21.900
in the dust and I looked up and I was looking&nbsp;
at a table of emperors of the secret societies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:21.900 --> 01:15:28.560
who wanted to know how I knew what I knew.&nbsp;
And I shared with them the iconography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:28.560 --> 01:15:35.640
passwords, handshakes, but it was all too&nbsp;
much and too little at the same time. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:35.640 --> 01:15:39.240
it was a very awkward moment and I had&nbsp;
to do something. I was there by myself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:40.200 --> 01:15:45.780
And there's always a human skull with a candle&nbsp;
burning with a bottle of raw sugar cane alcohol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:45.780 --> 01:15:49.680
the base of the poteau mitan, which&nbsp;
is used as a libation, not to drink.&nbsp;

01:15:49.680 --> 01:15:55.020
So I just thought, I better do something. So I&nbsp;
very deliberately went over, took the bottle and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:55.020 --> 01:16:01.020
poured the alcohol all over my body, my back, my&nbsp;
hair, everything. And then very deliberately went&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:01.020 --> 01:16:06.780
to the candle and lit myself on fire. And as&nbsp;
I flamed like a torch — remember, this isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:06.780 --> 01:16:10.980
kerosene or diesel, it's just like doing the same&nbsp;
thing with lighter fluid. There wasn't any danger&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:10.980 --> 01:16:16.920
except I'd lose my eyebrows. But while I was&nbsp;
still a living torch, I went over and offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:16.920 --> 01:16:22.440
the secret society handshake again to each of the&nbsp;
men. They loved it. Cracked up laughing. And after&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:22.440 --> 01:16:29.640
that I couldn't go by that crossroads without&nbsp;
getting flagged down. [Haitian Creole]. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:29.640 --> 01:16:34.200
became very friendly with that temple, actually.
Tim Ferriss: Well, okay, so hold on. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:34.200 --> 01:16:39.480
that's a hell of a party trick. So how&nbsp;
did this occur to you? And I guess you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:39.480 --> 01:16:44.040
just had knowledge that that wouldn't pose any&nbsp;
grave danger. I certainly wouldn't. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:44.040 --> 01:16:48.060
having your body on fire seems dangerous.
Wade Davis: But no, you must have lit your fingers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:48.060 --> 01:16:52.560
on fire with lighter fluid when you were a boy.
Tim Ferriss: I haven't. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:52.560 --> 01:16:55.920
maybe I should. Never say never.
Wade Davis: Tim, maybe it's a Canadian&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:55.920 --> 01:17:03.540
thing. I don't know. There are these moments, and&nbsp;
I don't mean in any way, because I keep saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:03.540 --> 01:17:10.920
"It's empathy and love, it's not bravado."&nbsp;
But I remember when I was 14, my mother was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:11.760 --> 01:17:17.520
a modest but determined Canadian woman, and she&nbsp;
worked all year as a secretary to raise enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:17.520 --> 01:17:22.500
money to let me join a group of kids a language&nbsp;
teacher was taking to Colombia. And I was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:22.500 --> 01:17:28.800
lucky because I was billeted in the mountains and&nbsp;
I never saw the other Canadian lads. And many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:28.800 --> 01:17:33.480
them got what the Colombians called mamitas, or&nbsp;
homesickness. And I felt like I just finally found&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:33.480 --> 01:17:39.060
home. I got drunk for the first time and kissed&nbsp;
a girl. I was in Heaven. But there was this real&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:39.060 --> 01:17:45.960
bully in the valley. I was 14 and he was 17 or&nbsp;
18, and he kind of terrorized — he wasn't a bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:45.960 --> 01:17:50.280
guy, [but] he terrorized all the kids.
And one day he challenged me to what they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:50.280 --> 01:17:54.600
do is they put a cigarette — you put your forearms&nbsp;
together, put a lit cigarette in the middle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:54.600 --> 01:18:02.040
and the first one to drop their arm is the loser.&nbsp;
He put the cigarette on, and I said, "This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:02.040 --> 01:18:08.220
stupid, it hurts." He didn't understand that that&nbsp;
cigarette could have burned through my arm before&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:08.220 --> 01:18:14.100
I would drop my arm. And of course I didn't drop&nbsp;
my arm. And eventually he had to drop his arm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:15.060 --> 01:18:20.700
And that was kind of a gift from me to him&nbsp;
because he was never the same again. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:20.700 --> 01:18:24.840
that he was a different character.&nbsp;
He didn't have to do that anymore.&nbsp;

01:18:24.840 --> 01:18:31.080
And to this day, I still have that huge scar on&nbsp;
my forearm, but I don't ever regret having done&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:31.080 --> 01:18:41.220
that. And what is wonderful about doing this&nbsp;
kind of field work is the dance of culture. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:41.220 --> 01:18:46.440
do you find the rhythm? It's like dancing with&nbsp;
a woman for the first time. Sometimes it works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:46.440 --> 01:18:54.060
sometimes it doesn't. I always say to young&nbsp;
students setting off to the field, "What you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:54.060 --> 01:19:00.840
need to do is just act like you would if invited&nbsp;
to someone's home at Thanksgiving. Be polite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:00.840 --> 01:19:06.240
have good manners, self-deprecating humor, a&nbsp;
willingness to eat what's put in front of you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:06.240 --> 01:19:11.280
and sleep where you're asked to sleep."
I mean, Tim, food is power. It's amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:11.280 --> 01:19:22.680
how many people will so crudely refuse a gift of&nbsp;
food. If you're being given food almost anywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:22.680 --> 01:19:29.160
in the world, it means some child is probably&nbsp;
not eating that day. And even if you know, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:29.160 --> 01:19:35.340
there's been many times when I've known because of&nbsp;
the circumstances, that if I eat a plate offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:35.340 --> 01:19:41.640
to me, without doubt, I'll contract giardia or&nbsp;
amoebic dysentery, I always eat the food. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:41.640 --> 01:19:47.340
you can always treat the illness, you can never&nbsp;
rekindle the trust that you've shattered, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:47.340 --> 01:19:53.460
just between you and the person, but between that&nbsp;
person and the next outsider who will come along.&nbsp;

01:19:53.460 --> 01:20:04.020
Tim Ferriss: I'd like to jump back to rites of&nbsp;
passage for a second. Because I do feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:04.860 --> 01:20:12.360
young males pay the price for the void of the&nbsp;
no rites of passage. And women too, but they do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:12.360 --> 01:20:17.520
have that inbuilt, as you pointed out, rite of&nbsp;
passage, whether it's formalized in some type&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:17.520 --> 01:20:23.700
of societal context or not. I'm curious how you&nbsp;
have, if at all, thought about rites of passage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:23.700 --> 01:20:31.320
for your own kids or how you might suggest parents&nbsp;
think about rites of passage. Primarily for males,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:31.320 --> 01:20:34.440
but it could also apply to females.
Wade Davis: Well, I had two girls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:34.440 --> 01:20:39.540
and when they each got their periods,&nbsp;
they came to me, not to their mother.&nbsp;

01:20:40.500 --> 01:20:43.980
Tim Ferriss: Why is that, do you think?
Wade Davis: You'd have to ask them, but I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:43.980 --> 01:20:49.620
I had that kind of relationship with them. I mean,&nbsp;
what we've done, obviously, with our daughters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:49.620 --> 01:20:57.300
we've been able to take them all around&nbsp;
the world. And also, while they grew up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:57.300 --> 01:21:03.120
we own a fishing lodge, a very modest fishing&nbsp;
lodge, but very remote, seven hours from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:03.120 --> 01:21:10.560
nearest town, three days drive north of Vancouver&nbsp;
in Tahltan country. And so while they grew up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:10.560 --> 01:21:15.420
I was traveling a great deal for the National&nbsp;
Geographic, the two or three months we would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:15.420 --> 01:21:20.700
spend there every summer became the well the&nbsp;
family drank from for the rest of the year. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:20.700 --> 01:21:26.520
for those months they hung out with Tahltan kids&nbsp;
all the time. And so they have this unbelievable&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:27.180 --> 01:21:36.120
sense of the world. My daughter speaks Arabic,&nbsp;
one of them. I remember our house in Washington&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:36.120 --> 01:21:42.420
in particular was kind of a Grand Central Station.
Everybody was free to crash there, and you never&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:42.420 --> 01:21:48.360
knew who was going to turn up. I just had a&nbsp;
friend call me who reminded me of the day he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:48.360 --> 01:21:54.540
woke up there and found four Mongolians drinking&nbsp;
vodka at 6:00 a.m. in the kitchen. And one time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:54.540 --> 01:21:59.700
we had Nilda Callañaupa, this great friend of&nbsp;
mine, a weaver, really a national treasure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:59.700 --> 01:22:05.640
from Cusco staying in one guest room. And&nbsp;
I'd forgotten to tell my daughters that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:05.640 --> 01:22:11.940
friend of mine from Mali, Isa Mohammed, a Tuareg,&nbsp;
massive guy, was there for the Folklife Festival.&nbsp;

01:22:11.940 --> 01:22:17.400
So they didn't know he was staying there, and&nbsp;
he comes up from the basement guest room in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:17.400 --> 01:22:23.100
full ritual regalia, looking like Lawrence&nbsp;
of Arabia. This guy, this massive guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:23.100 --> 01:22:27.840
much bigger than me, he comes into the kitchen,&nbsp;
spreads his arms across the kitchen. My girls&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:27.840 --> 01:22:33.960
were eating Rice Krispies at the table, and he&nbsp;
just says, "Mes enfants, je suis là!" And they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:33.960 --> 01:22:38.148
just look up at this incredible African guy.
Tim Ferriss: Could you translate that, please?&nbsp;

01:22:38.148 --> 01:22:43.200
Wade Davis: Yeah. "My children, I'm here."&nbsp;
And they just looked up at him and they said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:43.200 --> 01:22:47.280
"Hello, sir, how are you? You must be a friend&nbsp;
of my father's. Can we get you some breakfast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:47.280 --> 01:22:53.760
please?" I mean, utterly nonplussed. So those&nbsp;
are the kind of initiation rites — I think for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:55.800 --> 01:23:05.160
boys in the American context and culture it&nbsp;
really is great to find ways for them to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:05.160 --> 01:23:08.820
away and do physical labor. This is why I&nbsp;
think there should be Youth Conservation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:08.820 --> 01:23:16.320
Corps in every state and national park. There's&nbsp;
nothing better than for a 15-year old boy with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:16.320 --> 01:23:22.740
all those hormonal spasms and all that pimply&nbsp;
face to just be forced to cut firewood all day.&nbsp;

01:23:22.740 --> 01:23:29.640
And these kind of opportunities aren't trivial.&nbsp;
They create the character very young. There's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:29.640 --> 01:23:35.280
reason whatsoever that our government in the&nbsp;
United States shouldn't be able to mobilize&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:35.280 --> 01:23:42.360
resources that would make available to every&nbsp;
young American boy and girl the opportunity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:42.360 --> 01:23:48.360
travel within America, to know another face&nbsp;
of America, another section of the country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:50.100 --> 01:23:57.480
Californians to Iowa, Kansans to Miami, and so&nbsp;
on. And give them work to help make us a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:57.480 --> 01:24:04.020
country, whether it's picking up plastic&nbsp;
or caring for the elderly, whatever it is.&nbsp;

01:24:04.020 --> 01:24:10.020
Again, giving young people a sense that&nbsp;
they're not the center of the universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:11.400 --> 01:24:15.600
That they live to help others.&nbsp;
That we do exist as a community.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:16.740 --> 01:24:22.740
That you have to be humble. And just because&nbsp;
you believe it doesn't mean it's true. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:22.740 --> 01:24:29.280
the democratization of opinion doesn't mean&nbsp;
that your opinion counts as much as an elder&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:29.280 --> 01:24:35.040
who has lived through life that you can't even&nbsp;
imagine. There are ways to make this possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:35.040 --> 01:24:41.340
but physical activity is the key, I&nbsp;
think, particularly for men. Which is why,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:42.720 --> 01:24:51.900
say what you will about the military, it has&nbsp;
done more good for more young men in its history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:52.800 --> 01:24:58.830
Not that obviously we would not criticize some&nbsp;
of the engagements, but you know what I'm saying.&nbsp;

01:24:58.830 --> 01:25:00.120
Tim Ferriss: I do.
Wade Davis: As an institution&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:00.120 --> 01:25:09.120
of the nation that stands for the nation. Young&nbsp;
people have to learn that there's something bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:09.120 --> 01:25:17.700
than themselves that they need to be loyal to. And&nbsp;
that's not necessarily a country, it's a concept.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:17.700 --> 01:25:25.500
It's the idea of community. It's something we&nbsp;
really notice, Tim, in Canada, which is no perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:25.500 --> 01:25:33.420
place. But one of the things that is so different&nbsp;
in Canada is that there is really a sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:33.420 --> 01:25:40.080
community. We really are a social democracy. And&nbsp;
it creates for a different way of life. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:40.080 --> 01:25:46.560
one thing, I don't want to belabor this, but in&nbsp;
America, universal healthcare is seen as socialist&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:46.560 --> 01:25:55.500
medicine, and healthcare in general is seen as a&nbsp;
uniquely medical issue. And that is to miss the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:55.500 --> 01:26:02.040
point completely. Universal healthcare, which we&nbsp;
have in Canada, has nothing to do with medicine.&nbsp;

01:26:02.040 --> 01:26:12.300
It has everything to do with social solidarity. It&nbsp;
has everything to do with every Canadian knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:12.300 --> 01:26:17.760
that they belong, and knowing that if their kid&nbsp;
gets sick, they will get exactly, and I tell you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:17.760 --> 01:26:23.940
it is exactly, the same care as any other&nbsp;
Canadian, including the Prime Minister. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:23.940 --> 01:26:29.280
I sometimes have to wait for medical&nbsp;
service in Canada, but everyone does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:29.280 --> 01:26:37.620
but no one is left behind. And that is one of&nbsp;
the reasons that we have a less highly charged&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:38.220 --> 01:26:47.640
society, why we seem to get along better.
Tim Ferriss: I certainly agree with that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:47.640 --> 01:26:54.360
And I also agree with the physical labor&nbsp;
component and the importance of it for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:54.360 --> 01:27:02.220
boys and young men and men overall. But I think&nbsp;
especially in that hormonal tidal wave period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:02.220 --> 01:27:08.940
let's just call it, from whatever it&nbsp;
might be, 13 to 18. And in addition to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:10.500 --> 01:27:16.440
transcendence of the self to a larger cause, say&nbsp;
in a military context, it strikes me that both the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:16.440 --> 01:27:23.400
military and the physical exertion of, say, having&nbsp;
to chop wood all day, serve a similar purpose in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:23.400 --> 01:27:28.740
that of shared privation, which is a term you hear&nbsp;
in a military context. And that is a group of boys&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:28.740 --> 01:27:34.920
going through some form of suffering together.&nbsp;
And it does seem to activate something in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:34.920 --> 01:27:40.800
male psyche that is hard to access otherwise.
Wade Davis: One of my most memorable experiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:40.800 --> 01:27:46.800
when I was 20, I was just come back from&nbsp;
the Sierra Nevada with the mamos. And Tim&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:46.800 --> 01:27:51.060
was going back to Harvard to get his degree&nbsp;
and I had a month off until he was going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:51.060 --> 01:27:55.500
return. And I ran into this crazy Englishman&nbsp;
who had walked from the tip of South America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:55.500 --> 01:28:00.540
and he was walking to Alaska. And he was&nbsp;
sending these dispatches to his newspaper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:00.540 --> 01:28:06.900
The London Sunday Observer. And I'm not sure&nbsp;
what he wrote because in 18 months of walking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:06.900 --> 01:28:14.460
he hadn't learned a word of Spanish. But anyway,&nbsp;
he hired me to guide him through the Darien Gap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:14.460 --> 01:28:17.204
the only stretch of this trip —
Tim Ferriss: That's very dangerous.&nbsp;

01:28:17.204 --> 01:28:22.560
Wade Davis: — that had no road. And he didn't&nbsp;
care that I knew nothing about the area. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:22.560 --> 01:28:29.520
was great because he cultivated misadventures&nbsp;
as fodder for his books. And in the course of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:29.520 --> 01:28:36.420
that journey — it was incredible. During the rainy&nbsp;
season, walking in swamps up to the neck for days&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:36.420 --> 01:28:43.140
at a time. At one point we got lost in the jungle&nbsp;
with three Kuna Indians for 12 days with no food.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:44.940 --> 01:28:52.140
I was down to 146 pounds. At one point, Sebastian&nbsp;
was down to 120. We had to carry the other Indian&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:52.140 --> 01:28:56.820
lad. And at one point when we were at the&nbsp;
wit's end, I just said, "We've got to go."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:56.820 --> 01:29:03.060
And I took the gun and I walked up this trail&nbsp;
and I ran right into a black jaguar. And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:03.060 --> 01:29:06.960
you ever run into a jaguar, they've got these&nbsp;
yellow eyes. They don't look at you, they look&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:06.960 --> 01:29:12.720
through you. You feel like you've been x-rayed.
And I just looked at that incredibly beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:12.720 --> 01:29:18.900
creature, and then it leapt off the road. And I&nbsp;
thought we had two weeks to walk to get to rescue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:18.900 --> 01:29:24.240
but we found a way to the end of the road&nbsp;
that day. And it was just like a miracle. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:24.840 --> 01:29:30.360
at the end of this extraordinary misadventure,&nbsp;
I got off the plane in Panama City.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:31.680 --> 01:29:37.620
I had flown out in this cramped Cessna. The girl&nbsp;
beside me had puked on me. Her mother turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:37.620 --> 01:29:42.900
around to console the daughter, she puked on me.&nbsp;
I only had the rotten clothes on my back, $3 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:43.500 --> 01:29:49.320
my name, and one bottle of beer this engineer&nbsp;
had given me. I arrived in Panama City with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:49.320 --> 01:29:57.420
nothing more than that, and no plans whatsoever,&nbsp;
but I had never felt more alive. I had been on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:57.420 --> 01:30:04.500
my own hero's journey and I had survived.&nbsp;
And that would be etched into my character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:05.340 --> 01:30:11.580
If I could do that, I could do anything.
Tim Ferriss: This may seem — well, it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:11.580 --> 01:30:17.160
random because you prompted it in a way. So black&nbsp;
jaguars are not common as far as I know. What did&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:17.160 --> 01:30:20.220
the Kuna Indians make of that black jaguar?
Wade Davis: They didn't see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:20.220 --> 01:30:23.580
it. I was way ahead of them.
Tim Ferriss: I'm just wondering, even if you told&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:23.580 --> 01:30:31.260
them, if that carried any special significance.
Wade Davis: Oh, for sure. I mean, nothing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:31.260 --> 01:30:37.620
accidental when it comes to that. When you're&nbsp;
with peoples like the Barasana and the Macuna,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:38.160 --> 01:30:43.380
their most profound cultural insight, one might&nbsp;
say, is their conviction that plants and animals&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:43.380 --> 01:30:49.200
are just people in another dimension of&nbsp;
reality. So their hunting myths become&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:49.200 --> 01:30:54.180
kind of a land management plan dictating how&nbsp;
people can live in the forest. So the shaman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:54.180 --> 01:30:58.680
he's not just a priest or a physician, he's kind&nbsp;
of like a nuclear engineer who goes to the heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:58.680 --> 01:31:04.860
of the reactor to reprogram the world. So there's&nbsp;
a constant dialogue between human beings and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:04.860 --> 01:31:10.440
natural world, so no event has a life of its own.
Tim Ferriss: I do want to, at some point, and I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:10.440 --> 01:31:15.900
saying this to remind myself and maybe remind you&nbsp;
to remind me, about getting better at writing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:15.900 --> 01:31:20.820
teaching yourself to write on multiple levels.&nbsp;
We'll get to that. But since you brought up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:21.420 --> 01:31:28.080
the people in other dimensions, effectively,&nbsp;
manifesting as plants and animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:28.080 --> 01:31:32.700
I'd like to discuss, it doesn't have to be&nbsp;
brief, we have as much time as we want, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:33.300 --> 01:31:41.400
the different origin stories of this brew&nbsp;
called ayahuasca, which exists in many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:41.400 --> 01:31:47.760
iterations used by many, many different tribal&nbsp;
groups and cultures at this point, and churches&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:47.760 --> 01:31:56.160
also, syncretic religions at this point.
But I'm wondering how you would explain the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:57.000 --> 01:32:04.740
development of this particular combination&nbsp;
of plants. And the reason I ask is that I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:04.740 --> 01:32:08.580
heard many different explanations for&nbsp;
this. So one is just trial and error&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:08.580 --> 01:32:13.500
over a very long period of time. Another, on&nbsp;
the opposite end of the spectrum, would be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:14.340 --> 01:32:26.100
"The plants told us." And I'm wondering how you&nbsp;
would explain the arrival at this combination of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:26.100 --> 01:32:31.380
say, vine and — DMT-containing plant.
Wade Davis: Well, first of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:31.380 --> 01:32:38.580
one thing I've mentioned about ayahuasca or yagé&nbsp;
is that the idea we often have of it as we go&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:38.580 --> 01:32:46.020
down to Iquitos, or Pucallpa, the healers of the&nbsp;
Shipibo, as if it's sort of a quest for personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:47.700 --> 01:32:54.900
liberation, personal well-being. That's always&nbsp;
been there in the traditional use of hallucinogens&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:54.900 --> 01:33:01.740
in South America, the traditional syncretic cult&nbsp;
of the cactus of the four winds and the curanderos&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:01.740 --> 01:33:09.180
who use San Pedro cactus on the coast. And&nbsp;
certainly the popularization of ayahuasca began&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:09.180 --> 01:33:12.108
with The Yagé Letters between William Burroughs —
Tim Ferriss: Right.&nbsp;

01:33:12.108 --> 01:33:16.200
Wade Davis: And Allen Ginsberg. And it&nbsp;
was Burroughs who turned up in Bogota,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:16.200 --> 01:33:22.800
goes to the Herbarium, meets who he calls Doc&nbsp;
Schindler, who is Schultes. And Schultes sends him&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:22.800 --> 01:33:31.080
off and eventually gets him ayahuasca in Mocoa.&nbsp;
And on that road between Sibundoy and Mocoa in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:31.080 --> 01:33:37.920
upper Putumayo, when I was there in the 1970s,&nbsp;
there were already individual healers sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:39.060 --> 01:33:44.880
working with the gringo trade, but also&nbsp;
working with individual campesinos.&nbsp;

01:33:45.540 --> 01:33:53.220
And of course all of these healing practices,&nbsp;
the ideas that the imbalance of the individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:53.220 --> 01:33:58.680
is treated through the medicine and&nbsp;
whether the imbalance is caused by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:00.540 --> 01:34:08.940
bad health, bad, poor finances, personal problems,&nbsp;
whatever. It's a balance the source that one gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:08.940 --> 01:34:14.760
to. But I mentioned that only to stress that it&nbsp;
is completely a different situation when you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:14.760 --> 01:34:21.000
into the heart of the Northwest Amazon, where&nbsp;
presumably these plants were originated, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:21.000 --> 01:34:30.360
preparations. So for example, one of the powerful&nbsp;
themes that is somewhat like what the Kogi do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:30.360 --> 01:34:37.200
this idea that human beings aren't the problem,&nbsp;
we're the solution because only through the human&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:37.200 --> 01:34:43.320
imagination can the wonder of the natural world&nbsp;
become manifest, that we are the ones who have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:43.320 --> 01:34:49.260
maintain the energetic flows of the universe.&nbsp;
We have this proactive role to play well in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:49.260 --> 01:34:56.760
the Northwest Amazon, it's very much that way.
I mean, the main origin myth that in one way or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:56.760 --> 01:35:02.460
another is shared by multiple cultures, speaks&nbsp;
of a great journey from the east up the Milk&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:02.460 --> 01:35:11.040
River in sacred canoes dragged by Anaconda.&nbsp;
And in the canoes are all the hierarchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:11.040 --> 01:35:17.160
the chiefs, the wisdom keeper, the dancers, the&nbsp;
warriors, the slaves, and also the three vital&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:17.160 --> 01:35:26.460
plants, coca, yagé, and tobacco. And these are&nbsp;
brought up the Milk River. And originally they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:26.460 --> 01:35:34.020
were brought up by the Ayawa, the four thunders,&nbsp;
these mythological culture heroes. And they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:34.020 --> 01:35:39.720
encountered a world of total devastation and they&nbsp;
turned that world upside down and brought order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:39.720 --> 01:35:46.020
it by destroying the negative forces. So this idea&nbsp;
that humans are responsible for the equilibrium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:46.020 --> 01:35:52.260
and then the Ayawas went up and became the stars.&nbsp;
And then the great mother Romi Kumu brought the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:52.260 --> 01:35:58.020
people up and the people settled each river.
And because each river was settled by a unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:58.020 --> 01:36:03.480
canoe, each language group are related to&nbsp;
each other. You can't marry within your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:03.480 --> 01:36:08.700
language. So one of the extraordinary things&nbsp;
in the northwest, Amazon is I is linguistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:08.700 --> 01:36:12.660
exotomy when you marry, you must marry&nbsp;
someone who speaks a different language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:13.260 --> 01:36:19.860
but the use of ayahuasca is not individualistic.&nbsp;
It's collective at these great ceremonies that go&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:19.860 --> 01:36:26.040
on for two and three days where the individuals,&nbsp;
the men, all the people are there, but only men&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:26.040 --> 01:36:34.380
take yagé. They go through two different&nbsp;
kinds of ritual paraphernalia, feather work&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:35.220 --> 01:36:42.420
by day by night. And they literally, by&nbsp;
taking ayahuasca, don't become symbols of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:42.420 --> 01:36:48.840
the ancestors. They become the ancestors&nbsp;
and they fly away to all the sacred sites&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:49.440 --> 01:36:58.860
to pay homage to the natural world, to maintain&nbsp;
the harmonic balance. So the critical thing here&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:58.860 --> 01:37:06.420
is that the use of the plant preparation has&nbsp;
nothing to do with any individual's well-being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:06.420 --> 01:37:12.480
but rather becomes a prayer ceremony for the&nbsp;
collective well-being and survival of the culture.&nbsp;

01:37:12.480 --> 01:37:20.760
And it becomes the mediator to the divine. And so&nbsp;
the kinds of things you see in the kind of gringo&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:21.600 --> 01:37:31.080
ayahuasca business around Iquitos is not&nbsp;
traditional in that sense. Now as to how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:31.080 --> 01:37:37.260
this knowledge was discovered, I mean there&nbsp;
are a couple possibilities. First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:37.260 --> 01:37:46.260
there is a species of Malpighiaceous vine,&nbsp;
Diplopterys cabrerana, which, it looks very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:46.260 --> 01:37:52.380
like yagé and does have DMT in it. So maybe they&nbsp;
saw that, then they saw the opposite leaves, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:52.380 --> 01:37:59.400
saw the Psychotria coffee plant opposite leaves.&nbsp;
Clearly there's experimentation going on, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:59.400 --> 01:38:06.180
it's not just with ayahuasca, take something like&nbsp;
curare. The remarkable thing about curare: it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:06.180 --> 01:38:12.420
a muscle relaxant. But to affect the muscles, it&nbsp;
has to get into the blood. You can drink as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:12.420 --> 01:38:17.400
curare as you want, and if you don't have some&nbsp;
kind of wound in your stomach, you'll be fine.&nbsp;

01:38:18.960 --> 01:38:25.380
How do you rationally explain that process&nbsp;
of elaboration? And you mentioned trial and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:25.380 --> 01:38:29.340
error. Well, I think statistically that is just&nbsp;
click exposed as a meaningless euphemism. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:29.340 --> 01:38:36.900
I mentioned that story about Schultes saying the&nbsp;
Siona-Secoya and the 17 varieties sing to you in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:36.900 --> 01:38:42.540
a different key. Well, whatever that really&nbsp;
means when the people say "The plants teach&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:42.540 --> 01:38:49.080
us," I'm quite prepared at this point in my life&nbsp;
to take them at their word. And the reason I say&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:49.080 --> 01:38:59.760
that is that these men, largely men also women,&nbsp;
but in terms of ayahuasca, these aren't sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:59.760 --> 01:39:06.060
random characters. These are true natural&nbsp;
philosophers who understand that flora in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:06.060 --> 01:39:13.200
ways that few scientists could ever aspire to do.&nbsp;
They have spent their lives in wisdom, traditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:13.200 --> 01:39:19.860
lineages that have been taking all of this common&nbsp;
genius that I keep talking about, we all share as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:19.860 --> 01:39:26.580
human beings and applying it to that challenge.
I mean, for example, I mean, just jumping away for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:26.580 --> 01:39:33.420
a second. When you go to Australia, you realize&nbsp;
that the entire purpose of life is not to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:33.420 --> 01:39:38.700
the world, but to do the rituals to keep the&nbsp;
world just as it was. Well, imagine how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:38.700 --> 01:39:44.280
would be learned if the people of New York City&nbsp;
had spent all of their existence putting all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:44.280 --> 01:39:50.340
their energy and capacity into understanding the&nbsp;
biological relationships of Central Park. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:50.340 --> 01:39:56.880
it'd be incredible, right? So when we say "The&nbsp;
plants teach us," I'm not sure what that means.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:56.880 --> 01:40:04.260
I don't quite know how it'd become operative,&nbsp;
but I do know, and I've had this experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:04.260 --> 01:40:11.760
myself taking any number of psychoactive&nbsp;
substances that you have insights that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:11.760 --> 01:40:19.800
become almost challenging to believe in the wake&nbsp;
of your experience. I never understood the glory&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:19.800 --> 01:40:29.640
of photosynthesis. I never appreciated the miracle&nbsp;
of this verse of life, this idea that water can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:29.640 --> 01:40:34.200
come together with carbon dioxide and create the&nbsp;
air that we breathe and the food that we eat.&nbsp;

01:40:34.200 --> 01:40:39.480
I mean, that's a poetic verse every child should&nbsp;
have to memorize. And no politician should ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:39.480 --> 01:40:45.300
be able to run for office if they can't recite&nbsp;
the formula of photosynthesis. But the point is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:45.300 --> 01:40:52.320
I remember I took — Tim and I discovered a new&nbsp;
species of San Pedro cactus in 1974 in Bolivia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:52.320 --> 01:40:58.320
and we took a big wack of it on the eastern side&nbsp;
of the Andes and knowing that it was safe. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:58.980 --> 01:41:03.900
Tim and Schultes used to say Tim and I ate our&nbsp;
way through South America. If anything back then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:03.900 --> 01:41:12.420
had a chance to get us high, we would take it.
I know we were crazy, we were kids. As Tim and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:12.420 --> 01:41:18.000
made ready to say goodbye to each other after over&nbsp;
a year and a half traveling together, we took this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:18.000 --> 01:41:26.580
extraordinary, Terence would call heroic dose of&nbsp;
this new species we had found. And we were up for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:26.580 --> 01:41:32.760
48 hours. And one point we just left the ground&nbsp;
and we were flying over the surface of the Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:32.760 --> 01:41:39.840
And I looked down and I saw the Nazca Lines. And&nbsp;
I became convinced that that's how that explained&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:39.840 --> 01:41:47.040
how these guys conceived those monumental&nbsp;
structures. But at that same experience, I saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:47.040 --> 01:41:54.420
Tim fly up like Icarus to the sun and disappear.&nbsp;
And I knew right then he was going to die and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:54.420 --> 01:41:59.340
would be dead in short order. In fact, from AIDS.
Tim Ferriss: That's incredible. And for people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:59.340 --> 01:42:04.200
don't have the context, the Nazca Lines, which&nbsp;
I've seen from the air, are something to behold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:04.200 --> 01:42:09.960
So you see these huge depictions on the ground.
Wade Davis: There was anthropomorphic figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:09.960 --> 01:42:15.420
that from the ground you can never make&nbsp;
out. But from 10,000 feet you see these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:15.420 --> 01:42:23.340
perfectly etched forms of spiders and monkeys.
Tim Ferriss: Monkeys and also someone they refer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:23.340 --> 01:42:30.060
to as E.T. because it looks bizarrely like&nbsp;
our modern day depictions of aliens. So go&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:30.060 --> 01:42:34.827
figure that one out. Question for you about the&nbsp;
historic use of ayahuasca. Is it accurate to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:34.827 --> 01:42:37.680
or let me rephrase the question.&nbsp;
To what extent was ayahuasca or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:37.680 --> 01:42:44.220
yagé predominantly used for, say, hunting or&nbsp;
divination purposes versus healing purposes?&nbsp;

01:42:44.220 --> 01:42:48.480
Wade Davis: Oh, I think obviously it depends&nbsp;
on the setting the culture, the moment in time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:49.080 --> 01:42:55.380
Certainly all of these entheogens are&nbsp;
used in the course of healing. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:55.380 --> 01:43:01.860
the essence of the shamanic art of healing is&nbsp;
the idea that disease is not caused by pathogens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:01.860 --> 01:43:07.680
but by imbalance it has to be addressed. And to&nbsp;
do so, the shaman must invoke some technique of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:07.680 --> 01:43:13.380
ecstasy to soar away on the wings of trance to get&nbsp;
into these distant metaphysical realms where he or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:13.380 --> 01:43:21.000
she can do their work of medical magical, mystical&nbsp;
rescues. So ayahuasca in that sense has always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:21.000 --> 01:43:26.640
I think, been associated with healing arts.&nbsp;
But again, in the context of periodic rituals&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:26.640 --> 01:43:32.580
and ceremonies, as I mentioned with&nbsp;
the Barasana that we filmed actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:33.780 --> 01:43:38.880
the celebration of cassava women,&nbsp;
the kind of fertility ritual there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:38.880 --> 01:43:43.440
it's a journey of the community, the journey,&nbsp;
the community comes together in ritual.&nbsp;

01:43:43.440 --> 01:43:49.740
I mean, this idea of communities coming together&nbsp;
in ritual doesn't have to involve these sacred&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:49.740 --> 01:43:55.560
plants. I mean, in the Andes, for example, in the&nbsp;
community of Chinchero, once each year outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:55.560 --> 01:44:00.300
Cusco, the fastest young boy is given the honor&nbsp;
of becoming a woman. And he puts on the clothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:00.300 --> 01:44:06.780
of a sister and he leads all able-bodied men on&nbsp;
a run. But it's not your ordinary run. You start&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:06.780 --> 01:44:13.380
off at 11,500 feet, run 2,000 feet down to the&nbsp;
base of the sacred mountain, and then you run to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:14.100 --> 01:44:19.680
16,000 feet. And then you drop down to the sacred&nbsp;
valley and cross two more soaring Indian ridges.&nbsp;

01:44:19.680 --> 01:44:25.440
And you're running the boundaries of the community&nbsp;
land. But the wonderful metaphor is that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:25.440 --> 01:44:30.300
go into the mountain as an individual, but&nbsp;
through sacrifice, which means in Latin to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:30.300 --> 01:44:37.020
make sacred from pure exhaustion, you merge into&nbsp;
a single community that once again has expressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:37.020 --> 01:44:43.380
both its ownership but also its obligations to&nbsp;
land. I did that race when I was 48 years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:43.980 --> 01:44:48.660
the only outsider ever to do it. I trained&nbsp;
six months with an African American boxer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:49.440 --> 01:44:55.260
in DC in a gym. And I only got through the day&nbsp;
by chewing more coca leaves than anyone in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:55.980 --> 01:45:00.120
5,000 year history of the plant.
Tim Ferriss: But yeah, it does help.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:00.120 --> 01:45:07.140
I will say that of every remedy that was offered&nbsp;
to me that I certainly tested when I was suffering&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:07.140 --> 01:45:12.120
from altitude sickness in South America,&nbsp;
the only thing that fixed it and fixed it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:12.120 --> 01:45:17.035
very quickly was coca leaf tea. It was remarkable.
Wade Davis: It's a miracle. Well, I mean coca mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:17.035 --> 01:45:21.780
just without diverting ourselves from ayahuasca&nbsp;
prayer. But I mean, the thing about coca Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:22.380 --> 01:45:29.280
it's not just that coca is not cocaine, it's not&nbsp;
just that it's been misunderstood or whatever. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:29.280 --> 01:45:37.380
real tragedy is that humanity as a whole has been&nbsp;
robbed of the benefits of this incredible plant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:37.380 --> 01:45:46.260
If you go back to the 19th century when physicians&nbsp;
were studying coca, heralding its virtues in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:46.260 --> 01:45:54.180
non-judgmental context with open access to the&nbsp;
leaves, time and again, they would be befuddled&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:54.180 --> 01:45:59.700
by its activity. They called it the stimulant&nbsp;
that wasn't a stimulant. In other words, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:59.700 --> 01:46:06.240
would chew the leaves and you felt nothing except&nbsp;
the consequences of having done so. You suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:06.240 --> 01:46:13.680
felt a slight elevation of mood and the ability to&nbsp;
concentrate and focus, perhaps a drop in appetite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:13.680 --> 01:46:20.820
but you had no sense of the kind of charge you&nbsp;
get when even you drink a strong cup of coffee.&nbsp;

01:46:20.820 --> 01:46:26.760
And this, of course, is what makes the&nbsp;
plant so perfect for our modern age. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:26.760 --> 01:46:33.780
who wouldn't like to have access to a substance&nbsp;
that gently elevated their mood that was utterly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:33.780 --> 01:46:40.980
benign. It had 5,000 years of safe use that was&nbsp;
a sacred plant that allowed you to focus on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:40.980 --> 01:46:47.580
damn laptop without getting distracted to email.&nbsp;
That allowed you to overcome that slight inertia&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:47.580 --> 01:46:53.400
that keeps you from writing that first sentence&nbsp;
of your report. And then you suddenly discover&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:53.400 --> 01:46:59.040
at the end of the day, you've been doing this so&nbsp;
productively for eight hours and you just go home&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:59.040 --> 01:47:04.860
and you go to sleep, or you have your meal or&nbsp;
whatever with no side effects whatsoever. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:04.860 --> 01:47:09.420
you can do it again the next day and the next day&nbsp;
and next day. And before you know it, you see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:09.420 --> 01:47:18.300
your productivity, your well-being, your health&nbsp;
has soared. People always say to me, "How on Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:18.300 --> 01:47:26.760
have you written 23 books?" And I just wink.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, well, I'd love to open a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:26.760 --> 01:47:32.160
bottle of wine and talk about that wink sometime.&nbsp;
Certainly. Because I would like to do some more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:32.160 --> 01:47:37.380
writing myself. Let me come back to ayahuasca just&nbsp;
for a moment. Because you mentioned the trinity in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:37.380 --> 01:47:43.320
many of these cultures of coca, yagé, and tobacco,&nbsp;
and I'm fascinated by tobacco. That's a longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:43.320 --> 01:47:48.780
story for maybe another time, but they have read,&nbsp;
I think his name is, I'm probably mispronouncing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:48.780 --> 01:47:51.480
this, but Johannes Wilbert in his book.
Wade Davis: Oh, he was? Oh, he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:51.480 --> 01:47:53.328
wonderful. Oh, good for you.
Tim Ferriss: Amazing book.&nbsp;

01:47:53.328 --> 01:47:55.800
Wade Davis: He's a beautiful man. He just died.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:47:55.800 --> 01:48:00.420
Wade Davis: He was one of my great mentors.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, no kidding. All right.&nbsp;

01:48:00.420 --> 01:48:01.860
Wade Davis: Oh, yeah.
Tim Ferriss: So tobacco. Yeah. So tobacco&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:01.860 --> 01:48:07.320
is a whole separate chapter that I'd&nbsp;
love to talk about for hours. But another&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:07.320 --> 01:48:13.020
trinity I've I've seen mentioned is cassava,&nbsp;
ayahuasca, and then palm for different reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:14.340 --> 01:48:20.520
I have read a number of papers, or at least one&nbsp;
paper, I should say that in the hunting context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:20.520 --> 01:48:27.360
also mentioned use of ayahuasca&nbsp;
or dosing of hunting dogs with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:27.960 --> 01:48:34.020
ayahuasca. And I'm wondering if that can&nbsp;
be explained simply by hyper dilatation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:34.020 --> 01:48:39.540
of the pupils, maybe better hunting at night,&nbsp;
something along those lines, or if there's another&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:39.540 --> 01:48:44.040
explanation that people doing it would offer.
Wade Davis: Yeah, I mean, these things don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:44.040 --> 01:48:52.320
necessarily have a practical utility because&nbsp;
someone feeds their dog either Datura or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:53.700 --> 01:49:00.780
ayahuasca. Doesn't imply that the dog therefore&nbsp;
must get some attribute from that. That dose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:00.780 --> 01:49:06.840
I mean. It could easily be a kind of magical&nbsp;
idea or a metaphysical idea, a transcendent idea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:07.740 --> 01:49:13.200
But there is, there isn't that separation&nbsp;
from human beings and animals in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:13.200 --> 01:49:18.300
sense. So there's a lot of that goes on in the&nbsp;
Amazon. I mean, you mentioned yucca. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:18.300 --> 01:49:22.980
one of the fascinating things is that there&nbsp;
are many female anthropologists today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:22.980 --> 01:49:29.220
but in the early years it was obviously dominated&nbsp;
by men. And in the northwest Amazon in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:30.000 --> 01:49:35.400
there's a very clear division of labor. I would&nbsp;
never say that women are subordinate, on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:35.400 --> 01:49:42.420
contrary, but there's a very clear division of&nbsp;
labor. And for example, the gardens, the chakras,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:42.420 --> 01:49:46.140
are very much of the domain of the women.
I once made a mistake in the long house&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:46.740 --> 01:49:50.640
saying in front of all the men to all the&nbsp;
women. "Boy, I'd love you to take me to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:50.640 --> 01:49:55.920
garden and show me your cultivated plants." And&nbsp;
everybody laughed their heads off because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:55.920 --> 01:50:00.900
gardens are also where you go to make love. So&nbsp;
I'd essentially propositioned every woman in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:00.900 --> 01:50:08.460
maloca. But it does suggest that the realm of the&nbsp;
woman is not readily accessible to men. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:08.460 --> 01:50:13.800
I've often had people say to me, "All your&nbsp;
books, it seems to be a man's world." Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:13.800 --> 01:50:18.780
it's a man's world in the sense that I'm a man,&nbsp;
but that doesn't mean I'm not respectful of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:18.780 --> 01:50:23.400
woman's sphere. And one of the exciting&nbsp;
studies that was done with a Barasana&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:24.300 --> 01:50:28.920
by two great ethnographers, Stephen&nbsp;
Hugh-Jones and his wife Christine Hugh-Jones.&nbsp;

01:50:28.920 --> 01:50:35.280
While Stephen was looking at the Yurupary cult in&nbsp;
ayahuasca, and his book was published as The Palm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:35.280 --> 01:50:40.860
and the Pleiades, Christine was hanging for all&nbsp;
those years with the women. And her book, From the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:40.860 --> 01:50:48.300
Milk River, shows that the preparation of bitter&nbsp;
manioc is in wrapped in as much cosmology, as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:48.300 --> 01:50:55.260
significance as ayahuasca. I mean, if you think&nbsp;
about it in the Northwest Amazon, the main food is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:55.260 --> 01:51:03.060
cassava. And cassava's made from bitter manioc or&nbsp;
tapioca. And it's an incredibly elaborate process&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:03.060 --> 01:51:11.880
that the women have to do every day, transforming&nbsp;
a poisonous a root crop into the daily food of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:11.880 --> 01:51:19.380
their children. And it's not surprising that&nbsp;
the equipment and the process is absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:19.380 --> 01:51:27.000
celebrated in mythological terms. And that was&nbsp;
Christine's great contribution of that book.&nbsp;

01:51:27.960 --> 01:51:33.840
Tim Ferriss: So I will segue to the question on&nbsp;
writing because I selfishly also want to know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:33.840 --> 01:51:41.100
answer, but I would like to spend a little&nbsp;
bit more time on the sphere of psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:41.700 --> 01:51:49.920
with your experience of traveling over&nbsp;
experientially after taking this new species of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:49.920 --> 01:51:56.640
the San Pedro cactus, and then seeing your friend&nbsp;
flying into the sun and having this realization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:58.200 --> 01:52:03.900
How do you explain these phenomena, those types&nbsp;
of experiences, which seem to happen with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:03.900 --> 01:52:09.120
degree of regularity and shared visions,&nbsp;
at least purportedly shared visions with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:09.120 --> 01:52:11.760
ayahuasca and things at this time?
Wade Davis: Well, I think this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:11.760 --> 01:52:19.740
why human beings in all places for all time have&nbsp;
been fascinated by these entheogens because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:19.740 --> 01:52:27.600
really do reveal realms of ethereal wonder. And&nbsp;
as you well know in such a way that you almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:27.600 --> 01:52:34.140
are left feeling that the world, lovely as it&nbsp;
is that we dwell in, our ordinary consciousness&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:34.140 --> 01:52:41.640
is almost like a crude facsimile of a realm&nbsp;
that is beyond our imaginings at the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:41.640 --> 01:52:47.400
side of consciousness, if you want. And I think&nbsp;
that, as I've always said, I mentioned earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:47.400 --> 01:52:53.760
these sort of great social transformations&nbsp;
that can leave us, I think, hopeful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:55.200 --> 01:53:04.380
But when we look at the ingredients and the recipe&nbsp;
that allowed for those social changes, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:04.380 --> 01:53:09.240
one ingredient that we tend to expunge from the&nbsp;
record, which is the fact that millions of us in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:09.240 --> 01:53:17.340
that era, the '60s and '70s, took psychedelics.
And I don't think I would think the way I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:17.340 --> 01:53:23.280
I don't think I would write the way I write. I&nbsp;
don't think I would understand cultural relativism&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:23.280 --> 01:53:29.280
as I do. I don't think I would be drawn to nature&nbsp;
as I am. I don't think I treat women the way I do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:29.280 --> 01:53:37.500
understand gay men and women be as tolerant&nbsp;
and open as I've obviously am and have always&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:37.500 --> 01:53:42.420
been. If I hadn't taken psychedelics. I mean,&nbsp;
I always make this joke that her parents said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:42.420 --> 01:53:47.220
"Don't take these things, you'll never come&nbsp;
back the same." And the poor parents didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:47.220 --> 01:53:51.780
understand that was the entire point of the&nbsp;
exercise. We didn't want to come back the same.&nbsp;

01:53:51.780 --> 01:53:59.220
We wanted to come back transformed. I mean,&nbsp;
I think this was the key to my generation. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:59.220 --> 01:54:07.560
all suffered from Baudelaire's malady, a horror&nbsp;
of home. We grew up in a world that we found to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:07.560 --> 01:54:12.960
be problematic, or at least I did in terms of our&nbsp;
treatment of the environment, the way we treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:12.960 --> 01:54:17.940
women or the way we treated people of color,&nbsp;
the way that gay men and women were treated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:19.380 --> 01:54:27.720
And I went out looking for a more authentic&nbsp;
life in a different world. And so there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:27.720 --> 01:54:33.720
almost no separation between my desire&nbsp;
to know other cultures and other places,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:34.680 --> 01:54:40.680
and knowing other realms of consciousness just&nbsp;
went hand in hand with that. And I think in a way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:40.680 --> 01:54:49.620
looking back, this sort of became the multiple&nbsp;
elements of what was in effect my hero's journey.&nbsp;

01:54:50.280 --> 01:54:59.280
I set off with no plans except to be away&nbsp;
for at least a year. I had enough money to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:59.280 --> 01:55:05.460
stay with a budget of $3 a day. My only&nbsp;
promise was that I was not going to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:05.460 --> 01:55:11.700
back to the United States until Richard Nixon&nbsp;
was no longer president. And I waited them out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:11.700 --> 01:55:18.780
and I was gone 15 months. But looking back on&nbsp;
those months, it was an absolute initiation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:20.220 --> 01:55:26.640
And I came back a different person, which is of&nbsp;
course what one wants to do when one travels.&nbsp;

01:55:26.640 --> 01:55:30.840
Tim Ferriss: You have said that psychedelics&nbsp;
were useful to you when you were young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:30.840 --> 01:55:35.940
but later on more, perhaps, disorienting&nbsp;
and less helpful. Do you still&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:36.660 --> 01:55:39.720
feel that way? That's an accurate statement?
Wade Davis: Yeah, I think everybody, ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:39.720 --> 01:55:46.620
since Leary and Alpert and Andy Weil began&nbsp;
sharing this concept of set and setting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:46.620 --> 01:55:51.420
we've known that these substances kind of invoke,&nbsp;
they create a, they're completely neutral.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:51.420 --> 01:55:58.140
They create a kind of template upon which beliefs,&nbsp;
expectations, one's one set and the setting of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:58.140 --> 01:56:06.060
experience can play roles. And I personally&nbsp;
found, and I really believe in the Vedic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:06.060 --> 01:56:11.880
notion of the stages of life. You're a child,&nbsp;
you're a young man, you become a householder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:11.880 --> 01:56:18.480
and then you are free to wander as a sadhu, as&nbsp;
you approach the end of your life. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:18.480 --> 01:56:28.200
one wants to try to be in sync with those stages,&nbsp;
if you will. And when I was a young kid trying in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:28.200 --> 01:56:34.740
high school to deconstruct the world around me,&nbsp;
I loved to smoke pot because we were just you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:34.740 --> 01:56:41.400
know how it was you were 15, 16, just laughing&nbsp;
at the world and all the idiocies around you.&nbsp;

01:56:41.400 --> 01:56:47.880
And similarly psychedelics just opened my mind.&nbsp;
I mean, these are powerful forces. Let's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:47.880 --> 01:56:53.520
remember that because of psychedelics, The&nbsp;
Beatles went from "She Loves You" to "Tomorrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:53.520 --> 01:57:00.480
Never Knows" in two years. Think about that. And&nbsp;
then what I found, Tim, is to be honest with you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:00.480 --> 01:57:07.980
I lived such a crazy life. I mean, I didn't have&nbsp;
a home. I owned nothing but artifacts and books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:07.980 --> 01:57:16.620
I was on the go constantly. What I owned&nbsp;
was in storage. I was the amount of travel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:16.620 --> 01:57:25.860
it was all exciting, but at the time it was also&nbsp;
very kind of confusing. I was very ambitious to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:25.860 --> 01:57:31.800
know what my destiny would be. I knew I didn't&nbsp;
really want to be an academic. I love botany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:31.800 --> 01:57:36.480
but I wasn't going to be a botanist and so&nbsp;
on. And jumping ahead, it was when I wrote The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:36.480 --> 01:57:41.100
Serpent and the Rainbow that things clicked.
I said, "Oh, that's what I am. I can write."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:42.060 --> 01:57:50.460
But there was a very powerful year for me where&nbsp;
I was living in France after I'd finished The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:50.460 --> 01:57:56.460
Serpent and the Rainbow, but before it had come&nbsp;
out, and I was writing my PhD and living with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:56.460 --> 01:58:06.480
French girlfriend in a small village of 26 people&nbsp;
in Provence in the Alps, the Low Alps. And I got&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:06.480 --> 01:58:16.680
a phone call in the night that my father had&nbsp;
died. And I immediately came home to Canada. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:16.680 --> 01:58:21.900
that year, I can't remember the order that this&nbsp;
happened, but I had a letter waiting for me from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:21.900 --> 01:58:27.300
Gail. And in coming home from France, I walked&nbsp;
out of a relationship of five years with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:27.300 --> 01:58:33.660
older French woman, got home. There was a letter&nbsp;
waiting from Gail, who is now my wife of 35 years.&nbsp;

01:58:34.860 --> 01:58:41.940
Within a year, my father had died, I graduated&nbsp;
from Harvard after 18 years of it being my home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:43.020 --> 01:58:46.380
I had met Gail, she was&nbsp;
pregnant with the first child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:46.980 --> 01:58:52.080
my book had come out and I had made a&nbsp;
fortune, I bought a house with cash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:53.280 --> 01:58:58.440
and I was suddenly a father and a husband&nbsp;
and living in British Columbia in a home&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:58.440 --> 01:59:03.300
and writing a book about Schultes, having lost my&nbsp;
father earlier in the year. I mean all of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:04.320 --> 01:59:08.040
also, that year, the Hollywood&nbsp;
movie came out based on the book.&nbsp;

01:59:08.040 --> 01:59:14.040
It was like the I-Ching. My world turned over&nbsp;
and I found myself, once I became a father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:16.440 --> 01:59:21.240
a whole different set of priorities.&nbsp;
Now I had successfully built a world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:21.960 --> 01:59:29.400
I revered that world. I lived by that world, and&nbsp;
I didn't really at that critical time when I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:30.600 --> 01:59:38.340
a new husband, a new father, getting my career&nbsp;
underway, establishing a reputation as a speaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:39.060 --> 01:59:44.940
living in this little house on a hill,&nbsp;
writing this biography of Schultes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:44.940 --> 01:59:50.340
that I had no idea whether it would be successful&nbsp;
and become almost a cult book that it is.&nbsp;

01:59:50.340 --> 01:59:56.280
The point is that at that time, it was&nbsp;
not the time to be blowing open my mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:56.280 --> 02:00:02.280
right. On the contrary, right. It was a time&nbsp;
to consolidate, to take all I had learned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:02.280 --> 02:00:11.100
all my vagabond dreams and pull them together&nbsp;
as I kind of wove the fabric of my own&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:11.880 --> 02:00:19.440
individual life. And since then, I've taken&nbsp;
ayahuasca largely to sort of remember what&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:19.440 --> 02:00:24.240
it was like when I was writing One River.&nbsp;
And I've taken a few other things. I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:24.240 --> 02:00:31.440
easily find myself experimenting once again as&nbsp;
particularly as I get older and perhaps [am]&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:31.440 --> 02:00:37.080
approaching death. It's one of the things that&nbsp;
I think that these psychedelics are incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:37.080 --> 02:00:45.060
useful. Not to eliminate fear of death, but to&nbsp;
help make it seem natural and normal — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:45.060 --> 02:00:49.200
it is. So it's not, again, and I'm not here&nbsp;
judging. I mean, I think if you look back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:49.980 --> 02:00:57.720
Leary, for example, or John Lennon for that&nbsp;
matter, I would argue took way too much LSD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:57.720 --> 02:01:04.740
whereas George Harrison and Ram Dass famously,&nbsp;
as they wrote, got the message and hung up.&nbsp;

02:01:04.740 --> 02:01:14.520
In other words, I'm not sure how many times&nbsp;
you need to take these substances to learn what&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:14.520 --> 02:01:19.740
you're going to learn from them. Now that that's&nbsp;
me. I mean, other people find these to be part&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:19.740 --> 02:01:29.040
of an ongoing journey and engagement. One of the&nbsp;
things I do find interesting is how the gestalt on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:29.040 --> 02:01:34.500
ayahuasca has changed. If you had asked&nbsp;
me, Tim, I first took ayahuasca in 1974,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:34.500 --> 02:01:39.840
and if you had asked me then, which of all&nbsp;
the plants that I was becoming familiar with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:39.840 --> 02:01:49.560
would be the one that 45 years later would be in&nbsp;
every hallway of America? I would never have said&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:49.560 --> 02:01:54.180
ayahuasca. I mean, as Tim used to say, ayahuasca&nbsp;
is about many things. Pleasant isn't one of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:54.180 --> 02:02:00.240
And when you talk to the indigenous people,&nbsp;
it's fascinating. They use language like&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:02.400 --> 02:02:07.260
"You're the warrior confronting the&nbsp;
horror. You're nursing at the breast&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:07.260 --> 02:02:13.800
of Jaguar mother when she rips you from her kit&nbsp;
and flings you into a pit of poisonous vipers."&nbsp;

02:02:13.800 --> 02:02:19.860
I mean, once was with Randy Borman, and we&nbsp;
took ayahuasca with the Cofán and we had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:19.860 --> 02:02:29.880
very interesting kind of spontaneous session after&nbsp;
the experience. And I was asking, I said to these&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:29.880 --> 02:02:37.800
men I'd been through the journey with. I said, "I&nbsp;
got to tell you, this stuff scares the hell out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:37.800 --> 02:02:43.740
me." And they all looked at me, said, "Of course&nbsp;
it does. That's what it's supposed to do." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:43.740 --> 02:02:49.800
so what I find interesting is that people of your&nbsp;
generation and younger are all sort of reporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:49.800 --> 02:02:55.560
how kind of transcendent and blissful and&nbsp;
wonderful ayahuasca was. I mean, the last time I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:55.560 --> 02:03:00.240
took it, just to try to remember what it was like&nbsp;
to write about it, I remember sort of clinging to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:00.240 --> 02:03:08.280
my wife for about 24 hours. But anyway, I mean,&nbsp;
this is all in the realm of set and setting. I do&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:08.280 --> 02:03:15.060
find that I prefer something like San Pedro cactus&nbsp;
for the visceral connection to the natural world.&nbsp;

02:03:15.060 --> 02:03:22.680
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. I feel not that dissimilar&nbsp;
from your description, or the impetus behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:24.720 --> 02:03:30.360
The Wayfinders, that not polemics, not politics,&nbsp;
but storytelling is what you could use to drive a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:30.360 --> 02:03:35.460
change in culture. I think that ayahuasca,&nbsp;
maybe counterintuitively because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:36.300 --> 02:03:41.520
just awful experiences that many people&nbsp;
will have are challenging, let's just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:42.240 --> 02:03:48.300
has all the ingredients for great storytelling.&nbsp;
You have a group setting. You have, in most cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:48.300 --> 02:03:56.220
at least in the United States, an imported exotic&nbsp;
shaman who is running the show. You have shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:56.220 --> 02:04:02.820
privation in the form of vomiting and God knows&nbsp;
what else. And you have just the perfect cocktail&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:02.820 --> 02:04:11.820
for word of mouth in so many ways. And I think&nbsp;
for that reason it has traveled and become so&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:11.820 --> 02:04:17.400
sexy in a sense, unlike, say, mushrooms that in&nbsp;
the United States, these Psilocybe mushrooms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:17.400 --> 02:04:20.880
are taken in a very recreational setting&nbsp;
which would be very dissimilar from, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:20.880 --> 02:04:26.448
the Mazatec traditional use. But that never&nbsp;
made the hop, it never crossed the border.&nbsp;

02:04:26.448 --> 02:04:31.140
Wade Davis: I always wonder if — in the morning&nbsp;
after taking ayahuasca, I always just feel happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:31.140 --> 02:04:37.260
to be alive. And I'm sometimes with these young&nbsp;
people, where they're so happy just to have gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:37.260 --> 02:04:43.020
through it. But anyway, it's a phenomenon.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I think there's also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:44.940 --> 02:04:51.300
selection bias for the highlight reels.&nbsp;
It's much like if you're part of a religion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:51.300 --> 02:04:58.560
meaning I'm using "religion" in quotation marks,&nbsp;
that is dietary focused. And if your hair starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:58.560 --> 02:05:01.680
falling out because you're following some&nbsp;
weird diet, you don't want to confess that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:01.680 --> 02:05:07.020
to the group because you'll be ostracized.&nbsp;
So you just don't talk about how, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:07.020 --> 02:05:10.740
I felt destabilized for two weeks." But you do&nbsp;
tell them like, "Oh, I had this insight about my&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:10.740 --> 02:05:14.940
dad and things are so much better now." Anyway,&nbsp;
I'll put that aside. Let's talk about writing.&nbsp;

02:05:14.940 --> 02:05:27.420
I really enjoy your writing. It is poetic. It has&nbsp;
a very nuanced play of words and wordsmithing and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:28.260 --> 02:05:33.660
in the course of doing research for this&nbsp;
conversation, came across you mentioning on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:33.660 --> 02:05:37.620
several occasions that you were forced to teach&nbsp;
yourself to write well. And I'm most curious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:39.300 --> 02:05:42.900
not necessarily why that was the case,&nbsp;
but what the process looked like. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:42.900 --> 02:05:46.260
did you teach yourself to write well?
Wade Davis: It's good. I mean, a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:46.260 --> 02:05:54.060
aspiring writers out there. I mean, I wasn't aware&nbsp;
of myself having been anything like a writer in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:54.060 --> 02:05:58.980
say, high school. I mean, I later looked back&nbsp;
and was surprised to learn that I won the English&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:58.980 --> 02:06:06.660
prize and the history prize. And I did have&nbsp;
a tremendous foundation in English grammar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:07.320 --> 02:06:16.380
I went to a private school in Montreal in Grade 7&nbsp;
where we had to memorize the 120 sentence errors&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:16.380 --> 02:06:22.980
in English grammar. And literally we got whacked&nbsp;
if we didn't know them. That may sound silly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:22.980 --> 02:06:31.080
but grammar is the architecture of writing. I&nbsp;
mean, if you don't understand basic grammar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:31.080 --> 02:06:40.440
you can never be a writer and I have just an&nbsp;
incredible, intuitive understanding of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:40.440 --> 02:06:45.240
grammar of the English language because of that&nbsp;
experience when I was young and what happened.&nbsp;

02:06:45.780 --> 02:06:55.260
And I always kept journals when I was on the road&nbsp;
and I certainly was deeply impressed and I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:55.260 --> 02:07:01.740
mentors like the poet Gary Snyder. I never went&nbsp;
anywhere without one of Gary's volumes of poems&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:01.740 --> 02:07:09.180
in my backpack. Peter Matthiessen. A number of&nbsp;
writers that I really admired. And I was always&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:09.180 --> 02:07:15.900
drawn to the genre of travel books because&nbsp;
that's sort of what I was doing, who I was.&nbsp;

02:07:15.900 --> 02:07:24.660
But what actually happened with the case of The&nbsp;
Serpent and the Rainbow is, as I said earlier in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:24.660 --> 02:07:31.080
the podcast, the zombie research was funded&nbsp;
by Dr. Kline. They set up a dummy foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:31.080 --> 02:07:37.140
called the International Psychiatric Research&nbsp;
Foundation. And literally, at the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:37.140 --> 02:07:43.200
if I needed $5,000, $10,000 by Wednesday I&nbsp;
just had to call New York by Monday night.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:43.200 --> 02:07:48.540
And I never knew who was the benefactor, but it&nbsp;
turned out to be a wonderful man, David Merrick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:48.540 --> 02:07:55.380
the Broadway producer who at that time had just&nbsp;
had a huge success on Broadway with 42nd Street.&nbsp;

02:07:55.380 --> 02:08:02.580
And David had also done, obviously, a number of&nbsp;
feature films and he must have been hearing from&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:02.580 --> 02:08:09.180
Dr. Kline about my misadventures and he saw&nbsp;
a film from the very start in this. And then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:09.180 --> 02:08:16.080
again, as I mentioned earlier, in an unbelievable&nbsp;
24 hours, Dr. Kline died during routine heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:16.080 --> 02:08:21.840
surgery and Mr. Merrick had a debilitating&nbsp;
stroke so I literally went overnight from&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:21.840 --> 02:08:28.920
being flushed with support to having none. And I&nbsp;
did apply to all the standard research sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:28.920 --> 02:08:33.480
various grants, foundations, but they&nbsp;
all take months and months to let you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:33.480 --> 02:08:39.840
know whether you're going to get the money or not.
And I had guided that British journalist through&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:39.840 --> 02:08:47.520
the Darién Gap. His name was Sebastian Snow, and&nbsp;
he had written a really dreadful book about his&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:47.520 --> 02:08:55.800
journey called The Rucksack Man, and he actually&nbsp;
lifted whole passages out of my diaries into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:55.800 --> 02:09:02.640
book, which actually was a fair exchange because&nbsp;
it was the first time I saw my writing in print,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:02.640 --> 02:09:09.540
albeit lifted from my journals. Actually, I was&nbsp;
able to give him something worth saying because&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:09.540 --> 02:09:13.860
I spoke Spanish whilst hanging with the Indians&nbsp;
all the time. He knew nothing about where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:13.860 --> 02:09:18.360
was. He said if he spoke the Queen's English&nbsp;
loud enough they'd understand. He was just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:18.360 --> 02:09:23.580
completely eccentric guy. He went mad, actually,&nbsp;
in Costa Rica, but that's a whole 'nother story.&nbsp;

02:09:23.580 --> 02:09:27.900
But I thought, "Well, my God, if he could&nbsp;
write a book, I can write a book." So&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:27.900 --> 02:09:32.760
that was an idea. And then he generously&nbsp;
gave me the address of his literary agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:32.760 --> 02:09:38.520
in London. I walked off the street and said, "I've&nbsp;
got a couple of ideas." And this Englishman looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:38.520 --> 02:09:43.740
down his nose, as they often do to Canadians,&nbsp;
"Well, there might be something in that zombie&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:43.740 --> 02:09:49.380
thing." And before I knew it, I'd dictated the&nbsp;
story into a typewriter what happened to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:49.380 --> 02:09:53.880
had it transcribed, typed by Ed Wilson's,&nbsp;
Professor Wilson's wonderful, the late E.O.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:53.880 --> 02:10:00.780
Wilson's secretary. And I had this big 150-page&nbsp;
thing and I gave it in as a book proposal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:00.780 --> 02:10:08.280
got a contract for what then was an enormous&nbsp;
amount of money for me, $35,000, spent it on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:08.280 --> 02:10:13.320
some fun in Paris with a girlfriend, and then&nbsp;
used the rest of it to finish the research.&nbsp;

02:10:13.320 --> 02:10:19.260
And then I had to actually write a book. And I&nbsp;
wrote two chapters in Haiti. I had malaria and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:19.260 --> 02:10:24.180
hepatitis at the same time, and I was really&nbsp;
sick. And I wrote two chapters that I thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:24.180 --> 02:10:28.920
was the best thing since the Bible and I sent it&nbsp;
to the editor and he sent it back to me and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:28.920 --> 02:10:35.940
"Try again." And so then I left the university&nbsp;
and a very dear friend of mine plucked me out&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:35.940 --> 02:10:42.840
of Haiti and brought me to her beautiful farm&nbsp;
in Virginia to both get well but also to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:42.840 --> 02:10:48.240
the book. And I stayed there working&nbsp;
in a slave cabin for seven months. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:49.080 --> 02:10:55.620
I had a great story to tell. I had lived this&nbsp;
story, I just had to find the way to tell it.&nbsp;

02:10:55.620 --> 02:11:01.020
So what I did — and no one gave me this idea&nbsp;
and I certainly had never taken a creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:01.020 --> 02:11:04.740
writing course. I mean, most creative writing&nbsp;
courses are taught by people who are teaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:04.740 --> 02:11:11.100
creative writing courses because they can't write&nbsp;
creatively otherwise they'd be not doing that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:11.100 --> 02:11:16.080
they'd be writing books. But that may be a&nbsp;
little harsh. But all of us, lots of times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:16.080 --> 02:11:23.100
have to get academic jobs, don't get me wrong. But&nbsp;
what I did is I just took all my favorite books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:23.880 --> 02:11:30.540
Hemingway, for example, for dialogue. No&nbsp;
one's better. Isak Dinesen for landscape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:30.540 --> 02:11:37.920
Lawrence Durrell in The Alexandria Quartet. How&nbsp;
do you evoke the exoticism of a place as surreal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:37.920 --> 02:11:44.040
as Haiti? Well, how about Alexandria in&nbsp;
the 1940s? Carpentier for mystical thing.&nbsp;

02:11:44.040 --> 02:11:50.940
And this pile by my typewriter kept changing&nbsp;
and so on but it was always there and I never&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:50.940 --> 02:11:58.920
obviously copied or plagiarized, but as I was&nbsp;
trying to tell my story and I was stuck on how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:58.920 --> 02:12:06.300
do it, what language to use, I would just pick&nbsp;
up any book randomly and read for a while. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:06.300 --> 02:12:13.740
it was just weird. Almost like by osmosis I would&nbsp;
— and I was often writing at night. I had lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:13.740 --> 02:12:22.860
of coca and I was in this creative space.&nbsp;
And, again, one thing that I think a lesson&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:22.860 --> 02:12:27.780
of all that is in the end, I wrote that book in&nbsp;
seven months and it was edited in a single day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:27.780 --> 02:12:32.040
and it came out and it sold 500,000 copies.
Tim Ferriss: Hold on, hold on, hold on. You say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:32.040 --> 02:12:34.200
"Edited in a single day?"
Wade Davis: Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:34.200 --> 02:12:38.628
yeah. Almost no editing whatsoever.
Tim Ferriss: All right, I got it. Got it.&nbsp;

02:12:38.628 --> 02:12:42.000
Wade Davis: No, what I mean by that is, I mean,&nbsp;
look, there's lots of different ways of writing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:42.000 --> 02:12:48.060
Some people like to spit out a first draft, as&nbsp;
they say, or just puke it onto the page. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:48.060 --> 02:12:52.560
I've just never understood how you could&nbsp;
make something beautiful of puke. So I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:52.560 --> 02:13:00.540
to my detriment perhaps, am a much more laborious&nbsp;
writer and I've never done a second draft of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:00.540 --> 02:13:07.260
book. I do that in a way as I'm going through,&nbsp;
as I write a paragraph, I'm paying attention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:07.260 --> 02:13:14.460
I think writing's analogous to sculpture, you've&nbsp;
got to pay attention. Barbara Tuchman, the great&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:14.460 --> 02:13:21.060
historian, had a little note above her typewriter,&nbsp;
"Will they turn the page?" And you have to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:21.060 --> 02:13:26.220
rhythms in every line. You have to end every&nbsp;
paragraph with something that's going to make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:26.220 --> 02:13:32.400
reader want — and it becomes unconscious, right?
But at the same time you're paying attention to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:32.400 --> 02:13:37.920
the cadence of every word, at least I am. And&nbsp;
that's when you talk about lyricism and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:37.920 --> 02:13:43.080
was a skill I had to learn. In the early days&nbsp;
— it's very funny, my wife wouldn't bother to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:43.080 --> 02:13:47.460
edit or look at anything I write anymore, we've&nbsp;
been married too long, but in the early days she&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:47.460 --> 02:13:55.500
was wonderful at not editing per se. She never&nbsp;
added any words or deleted anything, but she had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:55.500 --> 02:14:03.000
a little stamp that we called the puke meter. And&nbsp;
if I had a passage where I went beyond lyricism to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:03.000 --> 02:14:09.240
purple prose, she'd just stamp it, a little face&nbsp;
like someone throwing up. And that was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:09.240 --> 02:14:15.960
wonderful because she was a great reader and you&nbsp;
learned you can always go out on the lyrical edge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:15.960 --> 02:14:24.120
if you will, because you can always pull it&nbsp;
back, but you can't bring spirit to dry prose.&nbsp;

02:14:25.680 --> 02:14:29.820
It's interesting. I mean, like everything else,&nbsp;
you get better and better the more you do it. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:29.820 --> 02:14:35.790
I write bizarrely effortlessly. I mean, no one&nbsp;
writes effortlessly. Hemingway said, "Anybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:35.790 --> 02:14:41.580
who says they write easily is either a bad&nbsp;
writer or a liar." But I certainly don't — also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:41.580 --> 02:14:47.280
I've never mistook activity for results because&nbsp;
I've been self-employed most of my life and I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:47.280 --> 02:14:53.880
never indulged writer's block. Can you imagine&nbsp;
if a plumber came to your home, looked at you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:53.880 --> 02:14:59.280
and suddenly patted their brow and said, "Just&nbsp;
can't do it today. I've got plumber's block."&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:59.280 --> 02:15:05.220
You'd call the bloody police, right? No, writing's&nbsp;
a craft. You get up in the morning and you do it.&nbsp;

02:15:05.220 --> 02:15:12.120
But one of the things that really was amazing&nbsp;
to me, a real turning point in my life, having&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:12.120 --> 02:15:19.860
been raised in that kind of simple middle-class&nbsp;
world where creativity happens to someone else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:19.860 --> 02:15:25.080
The Beatles are creative, Leonard Bernstein is&nbsp;
creative, whatever. When I finally understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:25.080 --> 02:15:31.860
that creativity is not the motivation of action,&nbsp;
it's a consequence of action. If you don't do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:31.860 --> 02:15:40.200
you can't create. So that insight and acting on&nbsp;
the insight changed my life. I never sat around&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:40.200 --> 02:15:46.740
to wait for permission to write about any subject.&nbsp;
People will say, "Oh, I can't write about World&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:46.740 --> 02:15:54.000
War I because I'm a botanist." No, you apply the&nbsp;
same research skills that you used to write about&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:54.000 --> 02:16:02.760
the Haitian zombie to understanding the essence&nbsp;
of British culture in Edwardian England. And in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:02.760 --> 02:16:08.580
the same token, you can't be a photographer if&nbsp;
you don't take pictures. The way you become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:08.580 --> 02:16:14.220
better photographer is to take more and more&nbsp;
images and to study the work of the masters.&nbsp;

02:16:14.220 --> 02:16:21.180
That's what I always would say to a young&nbsp;
person. If you want to write non-fiction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:21.840 --> 02:16:27.540
find the non-fiction that you think is the best&nbsp;
going and pay attention. Don't just read it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:27.540 --> 02:16:33.359
study it. It's the same thing with music. When&nbsp;
you listen to any of these great characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:33.359 --> 02:16:38.759
from Jimmy Page to the beloved and late&nbsp;
Jeff Beck, just terrible he passed away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:38.760 --> 02:16:46.620
they always talk about how they mimed the work of&nbsp;
every other blues player before them. Practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:46.620 --> 02:16:52.800
practice, practice. I mean, Jerry Garcia never&nbsp;
had a guitar out of his hands, nor did Hendrix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:53.640 --> 02:17:00.900
So you just have to do it and then ask. It's that&nbsp;
classic idea. Do what needs to be done and only&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:00.899 --> 02:17:07.799
then ask whether it was permissible or possible.
One of the things that I've found, Tim, about life&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:07.800 --> 02:17:12.660
is that at every single stage of life there's&nbsp;
someone telling you you shouldn't do something&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:13.320 --> 02:17:19.500
and nobody wants you to change. In my case it was&nbsp;
like, "You're from Canada. What's wrong with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:19.500 --> 02:17:23.160
University of — why do you have to go to Harvard?"&nbsp;
"Wait a minute, you're supposed to be a lawyer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:23.160 --> 02:17:27.660
What's this anthropology thing?" "Wait a minute,&nbsp;
you're an...? What's this botany thing?" "We just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:27.660 --> 02:17:31.139
came back from the Amazon. What's this Vodou&nbsp;
thing?" "Wait a minute. How can you go work on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:31.140 --> 02:17:36.120
a logging camp? Your father just spent a half his&nbsp;
money sending you to this fancy school." "Wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:36.120 --> 02:17:40.140
you've been in a logging camp. Why should you&nbsp;
be a park ranger?" "Wait a minute, you..." It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:40.140 --> 02:17:44.760
always like that and you just cannot look&nbsp;
behind you. I remember our mutual friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:44.760 --> 02:17:52.800
Mark Plotkin, once told me in the early days,&nbsp;
"The problem with climbing up the flagpole is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:52.800 --> 02:17:59.760
that there's always somebody looking up your&nbsp;
butt." By the way, that's a classic Mark line.&nbsp;

02:17:59.760 --> 02:18:03.600
Tim Ferriss: That is a classic Mark&nbsp;
line. That is a very classic Mark line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:04.620 --> 02:18:09.780
Well, I must say, Wade, this is an extremely&nbsp;
enjoyable conversation for me. I'm taking a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:09.780 --> 02:18:14.340
of notes. There are many other questions that I&nbsp;
would love to ask, but I think I'll make it not so&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:14.340 --> 02:18:22.020
much dealer's choice, but guest's choice. So let&nbsp;
me offer three options for wrapping up and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:22.020 --> 02:18:23.087
I would love to have you pick.
Wade Davis: Sure.&nbsp;

02:18:23.087 --> 02:18:28.860
Tim Ferriss: So the first option, which is&nbsp;
actually a question that Mark suggested although&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:28.859 --> 02:18:34.439
I would also love to know the answer, and that&nbsp;
is, why has Harvard not maintained their role as&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:34.439 --> 02:18:40.259
a leader in ethnobotany? So that's option one.
Option two is you attended and spoke at Dennis&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:40.260 --> 02:18:46.080
McKenna's ESPD55 conference, and I've had&nbsp;
Dennis on the podcast as well. What can&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:46.080 --> 02:18:49.920
you tell us about the current and future&nbsp;
use of infusions by western societies?&nbsp;

02:18:49.920 --> 02:18:56.820
And then the last option, which just came to&nbsp;
mind because Kurt Vonnegut, I think, separates&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:58.080 --> 02:19:05.040
the brain vomit first draft and then refine folks&nbsp;
as swoopers and then I think folks who operate&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:05.040 --> 02:19:10.320
more like yourself as plotters, I want to say.&nbsp;
And I tend to write more similar to how you write,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:10.320 --> 02:19:14.219
not to compare my writing to yours, but&nbsp;
I tend to rework and rework and rework.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:14.220 --> 02:19:18.360
But that only works for me if I have a very&nbsp;
reliable outline. So I was going to ask you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:18.359 --> 02:19:21.120
about your outlining process when writing.
Wade Davis: Well, if you don't mind, I'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:21.120 --> 02:19:24.256
much rather talk about writing&nbsp;
actually, if you do the third one.&nbsp;

02:19:24.256 --> 02:19:25.428
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, let's do&nbsp;
it. Yeah, yeah, let's do it.&nbsp;

02:19:25.427 --> 02:19:33.540
Wade Davis: I think all writers discover their&nbsp;
own kind of methods by definition. And what I do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:34.380 --> 02:19:39.420
and the strength of my books and the way that&nbsp;
some people might even call them too dense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:39.420 --> 02:19:45.420
is that they're incredibly deeply researched.&nbsp;
The book we haven't really talked about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:45.420 --> 02:19:51.180
Into the Silence, which actually won the prize for&nbsp;
the top book in the English language, which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:51.180 --> 02:20:00.241
then called the Samuel Johnson Prize, that took&nbsp;
12 years. And in that process I bought 600 books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:01.200 --> 02:20:10.500
With either myself or my research assistant we&nbsp;
visited 57 archives, multiple journeys to Tibet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:10.500 --> 02:20:19.920
I had the spiritual autobiography, the namthar&nbsp;
of Dzatrul Rinpoche from Rongbuk translated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:19.920 --> 02:20:27.060
monks in Kathmandu. I spent weeks myself in the&nbsp;
monastery, established by his spiritual heir, just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:27.060 --> 02:20:35.701
to know what Rongbuk would've looked like in 1921&nbsp;
and so on. So just insane amounts of research.&nbsp;

02:20:36.359 --> 02:20:42.059
And then what I do is I go through all that&nbsp;
material, and in the case, obviously, of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:42.780 --> 02:20:49.859
the Everest book there's files of&nbsp;
original letters and reports. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:50.939 --> 02:20:57.839
I create what I call work points. I don't&nbsp;
read the books, I don't scan the books or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:57.840 --> 02:21:04.140
skim the books. I think I'd say I mind the&nbsp;
books. I know, for example, that I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:05.760 --> 02:21:12.660
deal at some point with how Edwardian&nbsp;
women or women during the Great War&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:13.380 --> 02:21:20.100
dealt with the experience of death, as one&nbsp;
example. Well, I know that in my head that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:20.100 --> 02:21:25.980
there so as I go through all the material,&nbsp;
I start constructing these work points.&nbsp;

02:21:25.979 --> 02:21:34.200
And that work point could be anything on a&nbsp;
theme, homosexuality in the 1920s, or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:34.200 --> 02:21:42.479
Buddhist science of mind, and the work points&nbsp;
can become a huge number. But then everything I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:42.479 --> 02:21:49.620
then subsequently source, I know there's a work&nbsp;
point sheet, a document to put it in, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:50.640 --> 02:21:56.460
And so then that's how I distill&nbsp;
all this research into a manageable&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:57.300 --> 02:22:02.760
set of sources. And when I write the book, I&nbsp;
never go back unless I specifically need to for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:02.760 --> 02:22:09.840
one point or something to the original sources.
And at the same time, I find, Tim, you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:09.840 --> 02:22:19.440
try this, that the act of doing that unveils&nbsp;
the outline of the book, right? I don't think&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:19.439 --> 02:22:24.960
you can simply, "I want to write a book on&nbsp;
coca. Oh, here, it's going to be chapter one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:24.960 --> 02:22:30.060
chapter two." I mean, these book proposals from&nbsp;
publishers are so idiotic because you can't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:30.060 --> 02:22:36.180
do that. The book unveils itself. I mean, I'm sure&nbsp;
you've had this experience. You think, "Oh my God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:36.180 --> 02:22:43.260
I've got to talk about this element of his life.&nbsp;
It's going to be boring. I'll have to cover it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:43.260 --> 02:22:47.400
10 pages." And you discover you can actually&nbsp;
say all that you need to say in a paragraph.&nbsp;

02:22:47.399 --> 02:22:52.439
Or inversely you discover, "Oh my God,&nbsp;
Schultes..." Perfect example is Schultes&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:52.439 --> 02:22:57.419
in One River. "Oh, I knew he was involved&nbsp;
in this rubber crisis in World War II. Oh&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:57.420 --> 02:23:01.800
it's going to be so boring but I've got to cover&nbsp;
it." Turns out to be the most exciting part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:01.800 --> 02:23:07.980
his career and of the book, right? So the books&nbsp;
have a way of unfolding like that. And I find,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:07.979 --> 02:23:11.506
and I know some other writers don't —&nbsp;
David McCullough, the great writer —&nbsp;

02:23:11.507 --> 02:23:14.621
Tim Ferriss: Incredible writer.
Wade Davis: — starts to write his books before he&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:14.620 --> 02:23:18.168
has even finished the research, which I can't do.
Tim Ferriss: I can't do that either.&nbsp;

02:23:18.168 --> 02:23:26.340
Wade Davis: I feel it's like I have to have all&nbsp;
the stuff before me before I begin to assemble&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:26.340 --> 02:23:31.487
it into the writing. And I don't think it's a&nbsp;
slow process. I actually write very quickly.&nbsp;

02:23:31.487 --> 02:23:35.100
Tim Ferriss: May I ask a follow-up question&nbsp;
though? When you have your work points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:35.100 --> 02:23:39.900
do you organize them thematically into some type&nbsp;
of, not chronological, but order that you intend&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:39.899 --> 02:23:46.500
to follow as you piece those points into prose?
Wade Davis: Well, yes and no. I mean, with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:46.500 --> 02:23:54.540
case of Into the Silence, for example, I had to&nbsp;
have one whole work point, as I call it, on just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:54.540 --> 02:23:59.580
the pure chronology of the battles because I was&nbsp;
dealing with all these men who fought all over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:59.580 --> 02:24:04.620
place. I had to just really know what happened&nbsp;
at each battle. But to give you an idea of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:04.620 --> 02:24:13.559
research, there were 26 men who went to Everest&nbsp;
on those first expeditions, '21, 1922, 1924.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:14.520 --> 02:24:21.960
Six of them missed the War. Sandy Irvine, too&nbsp;
young. Longstaff, too old. One a school teacher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:21.960 --> 02:24:27.660
another a diplomat. But 20 saw the worst of the&nbsp;
fighting. And there were many other men. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:27.660 --> 02:24:35.519
set out to find out where each one of those&nbsp;
21 men, it turned out to be, had been every&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:35.520 --> 02:24:40.667
single day of the four years and four months&nbsp;
of the First World War and I did it. I did it.&nbsp;

02:24:40.667 --> 02:24:44.340
Tim Ferriss: Wow, that's an amazing test.
Wade Davis: Do you know what? It might be fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:44.340 --> 02:24:50.160
Tim, sometime when we're not just doing this&nbsp;
kind of podcast, if you're interested, we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:50.160 --> 02:24:53.939
get on call together for 10 minutes, I could&nbsp;
literally show you some of these work points.&nbsp;

02:24:53.939 --> 02:24:57.120
Tim Ferriss: Oh, please, I would love that.
Wade Davis: I do it with my graduate students.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:57.120 --> 02:25:03.899
It's a really helpful way — and then&nbsp;
you have things pop up. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:03.899 --> 02:25:09.479
that question of women's relationship to the war.&nbsp;
Well, you could write out 10 pages about that and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:09.479 --> 02:25:16.799
never achieve anything like a single line from&nbsp;
Diana Manners who said that, "By the end of 1916,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:16.800 --> 02:25:22.248
every boy I had ever danced with was dead." Right?
Tim Ferriss: Right.&nbsp;

02:25:22.248 --> 02:25:27.180
Wade Davis: And you're looking for those. And the&nbsp;
other thing I call, Tim, is wow points. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:27.180 --> 02:25:33.180
really important. If you're reading along, like&nbsp;
I was reading Max Hastings, a great historian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:33.180 --> 02:25:37.980
I really love his books, he wrote a book&nbsp;
about the last year of the Pacific War and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:37.979 --> 02:25:43.259
the overwhelming dominance of the Americans and&nbsp;
one of the statistics that just blew my mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:43.260 --> 02:25:45.960
and I'll give you this as a quiz.
Tim Ferriss: Okay.&nbsp;

02:25:45.960 --> 02:25:56.580
Wade Davis: For every four pounds of equipment,&nbsp;
food, gas, bullets, grenades, everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:57.540 --> 02:26:04.740
per capita, the Japanese Empire of the Sun got&nbsp;
to a frontline soldier per capita, how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:04.740 --> 02:26:11.399
pounds did we get? And across 13,000 kilometers of&nbsp;
ocean. As an American, you're going to love this.&nbsp;

02:26:11.399 --> 02:26:14.568
Tim Ferriss: I have no idea.&nbsp;
I won't even hazard a guess.&nbsp;

02:26:14.568 --> 02:26:16.740
Wade Davis: No, come on, you've got to guess.
Tim Ferriss: So, okay, so give me&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:16.740 --> 02:26:18.719
the question one more time.
Wade Davis: Okay. For every&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:18.720 --> 02:26:20.684
four pounds of equipment —
Tim Ferriss: Four pounds.&nbsp;

02:26:20.684 --> 02:26:24.107
Wade Davis: — per capita, not — and that means&nbsp;
for all the stuff they sent to the soldiers.&nbsp;

02:26:24.107 --> 02:26:26.700
Tim Ferriss: All the stuff.
Wade Davis: Everything from&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:26.700 --> 02:26:32.460
Tokyo or wherever it came from. For every&nbsp;
four pounds the Japanese got to a soldier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:32.460 --> 02:26:37.500
how many pounds did America get to a soldier?
Tim Ferriss: Okay, I'm just going to throw&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:37.500 --> 02:26:40.608
a number out there. It might be&nbsp;
overestimating. I'll say 40 pounds.&nbsp;

02:26:40.608 --> 02:26:43.668
Wade Davis: Two tons.
Tim Ferriss: Okay.&nbsp;

02:26:43.668 --> 02:26:50.160
Wade Davis: You see? So, when I'm reading&nbsp;
along like that, I go, "Wow," as you'd go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:50.160 --> 02:26:56.519
"Wow." And you file it away. And before you know&nbsp;
it, you're writing a piece in Rolling Stone about&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:56.520 --> 02:27:02.701
the unraveling of America and you want to speak&nbsp;
about how extraordinarily powerful America was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:02.700 --> 02:27:09.479
industrially in World War II. They come back to&nbsp;
you, they come back to you. But the key thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:09.479 --> 02:27:16.259
I find, Tim, is if you're reading and something&nbsp;
blows your mind, that's why I call it wow points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:16.260 --> 02:27:18.408
it's going to blow the mind of the reader.
Tim Ferriss: For sure.&nbsp;

02:27:18.408 --> 02:27:20.880
Wade Davis: And you want to make&nbsp;
sure that you find a way to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:20.880 --> 02:27:26.100
all those wow points into your manuscript.
Tim Ferriss: How do you file your wow points&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:26.640 --> 02:27:28.007
or just make these available?
Wade Davis: In Word documents.&nbsp;

02:27:28.007 --> 02:27:30.360
Tim Ferriss: In Word documents.
Wade Davis: But yeah, and like&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:30.960 --> 02:27:36.180
Nancy Manners, I get that thing about, "Everybody&nbsp;
I danced with was dead." Well, that would be filed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:36.180 --> 02:27:41.928
away in Women in the War, something like that.
Tim Ferriss: Got it.&nbsp;

02:27:41.927 --> 02:27:45.540
Wade Davis: I'd be happy, off record, out of&nbsp;
office hours kind of thing, just when you got&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:45.540 --> 02:27:49.727
a moment and we might be able to pull them up&nbsp;
and it's good exercise. It's really helpful.&nbsp;

02:27:49.727 --> 02:27:51.975
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I would love to do&nbsp;
it. That's a very generous offer.&nbsp;

02:27:51.976 --> 02:27:53.322
Wade Davis: It's your only way.
Tim Ferriss: My answer is yes.&nbsp;

02:27:53.322 --> 02:27:58.020
Wade Davis: Well, it's the only way to&nbsp;
deal with the body of material. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:58.020 --> 02:28:02.161
this whole wall of my office here is&nbsp;
books that I bought for that one book.&nbsp;

02:28:02.160 --> 02:28:07.200
Tim Ferriss: Wow. Yes. So I would love to&nbsp;
do that. That's a very generous offer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:07.200 --> 02:28:11.399
you've been very generous with your time. I&nbsp;
appreciate you being so game to go two and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:11.399 --> 02:28:15.599
a half hours. I could go another two and a&nbsp;
half hours, but maybe we'll save that for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:15.600 --> 02:28:18.720
round two if this torture wasn't too bad.
Wade Davis: No, it's fine. And it's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:18.720 --> 02:28:22.080
a lot of fun. And you asked at the very&nbsp;
beginning. What you did is exactly what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:22.080 --> 02:28:24.168
was hoping you'd do. It was fun.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, amazing.&nbsp;

02:28:24.168 --> 02:28:27.347
Wade Davis: It wasn't work. It was a&nbsp;
conversation, my friend. Very kind of you.&nbsp;

02:28:27.346 --> 02:28:31.259
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I loved it. And I hope&nbsp;
we get to meet in person, but before then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:31.260 --> 02:28:35.520
we could certainly have a phone call. I would&nbsp;
love to see your work points and your flow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:35.520 --> 02:28:40.920
Is there anything you would like to mention,&nbsp;
call attention to? Any request of the audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:40.920 --> 02:28:45.240
you'd like to make before we come to the close?
Wade Davis: No, I mean, not really. I mean, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:45.240 --> 02:28:50.399
nice that you can plug the Magdalena book at the&nbsp;
introduction, but other than that, that's fine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:50.399 --> 02:28:55.620
Unless there's something you want me to say.
Tim Ferriss: No, no, you've done more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:55.620 --> 02:29:00.540
enough. And I will just say thank you&nbsp;
very much. And to everybody listening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:00.540 --> 02:29:06.540
we will link to all of the books, all of the&nbsp;
references, many, many. If not, we will attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:06.540 --> 02:29:10.800
to get all of the things that were mentioned in&nbsp;
this conversation in the show notes as per usual&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:10.800 --> 02:29:19.560
at tim.blog/podcast. And, wow, Wade Davis, ladies&nbsp;
and gentlemen. And thanks everybody for tuning in.

